he file carries the statement with a\n\\texttt{sorry} for its proof (line 218); the solution tree proves the same\nstatement, and the collection's comparator checks that the two agree\n(Section~\\ref{sec:proof}). The replay of Section~\\ref{sec:replay}\nrebuilt that proof and found the three standard axioms and nothing else.\nThis section reads the statement, definition by definition, and says what\nit asserts and what it does not. Line numbers are those of the comparator\nfile; we quote nothing from memory.\n\n\\subsection{The definitions, one by one}\n\n\\paragraph{Formulas and the count (lines 13--51).}\nA \\texttt{Literal V} is a variable with a sign (lines 19--22); a\n\\texttt{Clause V} is a \\texttt{Finset} of literals and a \\texttt{CNF V} a\n\\texttt{Finset} of clauses (lines 24--25). An assignment is any function\n\\(V \\to \\mathtt{Bool}\\) (line 17). A literal evaluates to the Boolean\n``the assignment agrees with its sign'' (lines 29--30), a clause to ``some\nliteral is true'' (lines 32--33), a formula to ``every clause is true''\n(lines 35--36). \\texttt{WidthAtMost k F} says every clause has at most\n\\(k\\) literals as a set (line 38). Over a finite variable type,\n\\texttt{satisfyingAssignments F} filters the universe of all assignments\n(lines 44--45), and \\texttt{count F} is its cardinality (line 47): the\nnumber of satisfying assignments among all \\(2^n\\) when\n\\(V = \\mathtt{Fin}\\ n\\). The \\texttt{Finset} choices change nothing in the\nmeaning: a repeated clause or literal collapses, the empty clause is\nallowed and unsatisfiable, a tautological clause \\(\\{x, \\neg x\\}\\) is\nallowed and counts two toward the width. That is consistent with the\nmanuscript's ``Repeating a literal permits clauses with fewer than \\(k\\)\nliterals'' (p.~1, line~44).\n\n\\paragraph{The input encoding (lines 53--81).}\n\\texttt{encodeNat n} is \\(n\\) ones followed by a zero (line 55): unary.\n\\texttt{frame} doubles a bit string, prefixing each bit with a one, and\nends it with a zero (lines 57--58). \\texttt{encodeList enc} prefixes each\nelement's frame with a one and ends the list with a zero (lines 60--62).\nA literal becomes the number \\(2\\cdot\\mathrm{var} + [\\text{positive}]\\),\nat most \\(2n - 1\\) (lines 64--65); a clause is its sorted list of literal\nnumbers, a formula its sorted list of clauses (lines 67--71).\n\\texttt{encodeCNF F} is \\texttt{encodeNat n} followed by the formula's\nlist of lists of unary numbers (lines 73--77), and\n\\texttt{formulaBitLength F} is the length of that bit string (line 79).\nTwo things follow. The number of variables \\(n\\) is part of the input,\nso a variable that occurs in no clause is still counted. And the length\nis polynomial: a clause of \\(j \\le k\\) literals costs \\(2j(4n+1)+4\\) bits,\nso\n\\[\n  L \\;=\\; \\mathtt{formulaBitLength}\\ F \\;\\le\\; n + 2 + m\\,\\bigl(2k(4n+1) + 4\\bigr)\n  \\;=\\; O(k\\,n\\,m)\n\\]\nfor \\(m\\) clauses. The solution itself proves\n\\((L+1)^d \\le (8k+7)^d\\,(n + |F| + 1)^{2d}\\)\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Statement.lean}, lines 9--14). Emulating the\ndefinitions (\\repofile{reports/scratch/eth_audit/check_encoding.py}),\na formula with \\(n = 125\\), \\(m = 500\\), \\(k = 4\\) encodes to\n\\(L = 1{,}006{,}223\\) bits and one with \\(n = 1{,}000\\), \\(m = 4{,}000\\),\n\\(k = 3\\) to \\(47.9\\) million, both under the bound. Nothing in the\nencoding is exponential; the unary numbers are linear in \\(n\\).\n\n\\paragraph{The machine (lines 83--128).}\n\\texttt{PrimitiveMachine} (lines 92--99) is a finite object: a number\n\\texttt{tapeCount} of tapes and a number \\texttt{stateCount} of states,\nan input tape and a start state, a table \\texttt{scan} saying which tape\neach state reads, and a table \\texttt{transition} from\n\\(\\mathtt{Fin}\\ \\mathtt{stateCount} \\times \\mathtt{Option\\ Bool}\\)---a\ndomain of \\(3 \\cdot \\mathtt{stateCount}\\) points---into either a halting\nanswer (a \\texttt{Bool}) or a next state with one action. The action type\nis Mathlib's \\texttt{Turing.TM0.Stmt (Option Bool)}: move the head left or\nright, or write one of the three symbols \\texttt{none}, \\texttt{some\nfalse}, \\texttt{some true}, five values in all\n(\\path{Mathlib/Computability/TuringMachine/PostTuringMachine.lean}\nlines 118--120 and \\path{Tape.lean} lines 421--423 at Mathlib\n\\texttt{d13f23b7}, the clone's pinned revision). A configuration\n\\texttt{Cfg} (lines 103--105) is a state, halted or not, and one Mathlib\n\\texttt{Tape} per tape index (a head symbol and two blank-padded halves,\n\\path{Tape.lean} lines 408--414, blank \\(=\\) \\texttt{none}).\n\\texttt{step} (lines 107--119) returns \\texttt{none} in a halted state;\notherwise it reads the head of the one tape the state scans, looks the\npair up in the table, and either halts with the answer, or writes one\nsymbol or moves that one head by one cell and changes state. \\texttt{init}\n(lines 121--124) puts the input on the input tape with the head at its\nfirst bit and every other tape blank (\\texttt{Tape.mk}$_1$,\n\\path{Tape.lean} line 494). A Lean function on a finite domain is,\nwhatever term defines it, a finite table; so a step is one lookup in a\ntable of \\(3 \\cdot \\mathtt{stateCount}\\) rows, and no field of the\nstructure can carry unbounded computation.\n\n\\paragraph{Traces (lines 87--90).}\n\\texttt{Trace step t a c} is an inductive proposition with two\nconstructors: \\texttt{refl}, a trace of length 0 from \\(a\\) to \\(a\\)\nitself, and \\texttt{cons}, which extends a trace from \\(b\\) by one\nsuccessful step \\(a \\mapsto b\\). So it holds exactly when \\(c\\) is\nreached from \\(a\\) by \\(t\\) successful applications of \\texttt{step}, and\na trace of length 0 to a different configuration is impossible. Since\n\\texttt{step} is a function, the run is deterministic.\n\n\\paragraph{Space monitoring (lines 147--189).}\n\\texttt{LocatedCfg} (lines 149--153) pairs a configuration with, per tape,\nthe head's current position and the least and greatest positions seen so\nfar, as integers; \\texttt{locatedInit} starts every head at 0 with the\ninput tape's range covering the input (lines 155--159);\n\\texttt{headDisplacement} is \\(-1\\), \\(+1\\) or \\(0\\) for a move left, a\nmove right or a write (lines 161--164); \\texttt{monitor} (lines 166--182)\napplies the same table lookup to update the position and the running\nminimum and maximum of the scanned tape, and nothing else;\n\\texttt{locatedStep} is \\texttt{step} on the configuration followed by\n\\texttt{monitor} (lines 184--186). \\texttt{visitedSpace} (lines 188--189)\nis the sum over tapes of \\(\\mathrm{upper} - \\mathrm{lower} + 1\\): the\nnumber of cells whose head position was ever visited. \\texttt{countWord a}\nis the binary expansion of \\(a\\), least significant bit first, with\n\\texttt{[false]} for \\(0\\) (line 147), and \\texttt{toNat} (lines 7--9)\ndecodes such a word.\n\n\\paragraph{The exponent and the bound (lines 130--141).}\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n\\texttt{runtimeScale n = n * (Real.log (Real.log (n + 16)))\\^{}2 /}\\\\\n\\texttt{\\quad Real.sqrt (Real.log (n + 16))}\n\\end{quote}\n(lines 132--134, transcribed; the casts to \\(\\mathbb{R}\\) omitted), that is\n\\[\n  \\rhon \\;=\\; \\frac{n\\,\\bigl(\\ln\\ln(n+16)\\bigr)^2}{\\sqrt{\\ln(n+16)}},\n\\]\nwith \\texttt{Real.log} the natural logarithm. The manuscript fixes the\nsame base: ``Throughout, log denotes the natural logarithm'' (p.~1, lines\n48--49; again at p.~7, lines 326--327). The shift by 16 keeps\n\\(\\ln\\ln\\) defined and positive at every \\(n \\ge 0\\) (\\(\\ln 16 = 2.77\\)),\nso the theorem can be stated for all \\(n\\) with no proviso. Then\n\\begin{quote}\n\\texttt{runtimeBound C A d L n = C * (L + 1)\\^{}d * 2\\^{}(A * runtimeScale n)}\n\\end{quote}\n(lines 136--139, transcribed), a real number, with the real-exponent\npower. We write \\(\\rhon\\) for \\texttt{runtimeScale n} throughout the\npaper.\n\n\\subsection{The theorem, and the order of its quantifiers}\n\nLines 194--217 state, for every \\(k\\) with \\(2 \\le k\\):\n\\[\n\\small\n\\begin{array}{l}\n\\exists\\ \\mathtt{machine},\\ \\mathtt{countTape},\\ \\mathtt{answerTape},\\\n  C, A, S \\in \\mathbb{R},\\ d, e \\in \\mathbb{N},\\\\[2pt]\n\\quad 0 < C \\ \\wedge\\ 0 < A \\ \\wedge\\ 0 < S \\ \\wedge\\ 0 < d \\ \\wedge\\ 0 < e\\ \\wedge\\\\[2pt]\n\\quad \\forall\\, n \\in \\mathbb{N},\\ \\forall\\, F : \\mathtt{CNF}\\ (\\mathtt{Fin}\\ n),\\\n  \\mathtt{WidthAtMost}\\ k\\ F \\;\\rightarrow\\\\[2pt]\n\\qquad \\exists\\ \\mathtt{time} \\in \\mathbb{N},\\ \\mathtt{final} : \\mathtt{LocatedCfg}\\ \\mathtt{machine},\\\\[2pt]\n\\qquad\\quad \\mathtt{Trace}\\ (\\mathtt{locatedStep}\\ \\mathtt{machine})\\ \\mathtt{time}\\\n  (\\mathtt{locatedInit}\\ \\mathtt{machine}\\ (\\mathtt{encodeCNF}\\ F))\\ \\mathtt{final}\\\\[2pt]\n\\qquad\\qquad\\qquad\\text{(lines 201--202)}\\\\[2pt]\n\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ \\text{the count tape holds \\texttt{countWord (count F)}}\n  \\quad\\text{(203--204)}\\\\[2pt]\n\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ \\text{the answer tape holds the bit \\([0 < \\mathtt{count}\\ F]\\)}\n  \\quad\\text{(205--206)}\\\\[2pt]\n\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ \\text{the halting state carries that bit}\n  \\quad\\text{(207)}\\\\[2pt]\n\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ \\mathtt{toNat}\\ (\\mathtt{countWord}\\ (\\mathtt{count}\\ F)) = \\mathtt{count}\\ F\n  \\quad\\text{(208)}\\\\[2pt]\n\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ |\\mathtt{countWord}\\ (\\mathtt{count}\\ F)| \\le n + 1\n  \\quad\\text{(209)}\\\\[2pt]\n\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ \\mathtt{time} \\le C\\,(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\,\\rhon},\\ L = \\mathtt{formulaBitLength}\\ F\n  \\quad\\text{(210--211)}\\\\[2pt]\n\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ \\forall\\ \\text{prefix of the trace},\\\n  \\mathtt{visitedSpace} \\le S\\,(L+1)^e\n  \\quad\\text{(212--217).}\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nThree features of the order matter.\n\n\\emph{The machine and the five constants come after \\(k\\) and before\n\\(n\\) and \\(F\\).} They may depend on \\(k\\) and on nothing else; inside\nthe universal quantifier only \\texttt{time} and \\texttt{final} are\nchosen. So for each \\(k\\) one fixed finite machine serves every formula\nof every size, and \\(C, A, S, d, e\\) are the same for all of them: the\nstandard \\(O_k(1)\\) reading. The table's size is \\(O_k(1)\\) in the same\nsense, with no explicit function of \\(k\\).\n\n\\emph{There is no lower bound on \\(n\\).} The manuscript's Theorem~1.1\nand Proposition~6.4 are stated for \\(n \\ge 20\\) (p.~2, lines 56--57;\np.~43, lines 2474--2475), and the solution's auxiliary\n\\texttt{exact\\_count\\_eth} keeps \\(20 \\le n\\)\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}, lines 104--109); the exported\ntheorem does not. The \\(+16\\) in \\(\\rhon\\) is what makes that possible.\n\n\\emph{The constants are existential.} The statement asserts that values\nof \\(C, A, S, d, e\\) exist; it names none. The proof obtains them from\nexistential lemmas and never evaluates them: at the point where the\nlarge-input run is assembled, the exponent constant is set to\n\\(A = 256 + A_t\\) with \\(A_t \\ge 1\\) taken from a leaf contract\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLargeEntry.lean}, line 51;\n\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLeafContract.lean}, lines 792--793), and\nthe degree and the polynomial constants are built the same way. So the\nproof as written has \\(A \\ge 257\\), and no closed value of any of the\nfive. The witness machine is explicit in one respect: it is compiled\nfrom a program over 80 one-bit registers and 1,220 tapes, with\n\\(\\mathtt{countTape} = 256\\) and \\(\\mathtt{answerTape} = 1216\\)\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}, lines 98--101); its state count is\na finite type's cardinality that nothing computes. Section~\\ref{sec:proof}\nis the inspection of the proof; this section only reads what the\nstatement fixes and what it leaves open.\n\n\\subsection{The exponent, in numbers}\n\nEverything the title says rests on one function. With \\(y = \\ln(n+16)\\),\n\\[\n  \\frac{\\rhon}{n} \\;=\\; \\frac{(\\ln y)^2}{\\sqrt{y}},\n\\]\nwhich rises from \\(0.64\\) at \\(n = 1\\), crosses \\(1\\) between\n\\(n = 49\\) and \\(n = 50\\), peaks at \\(16/e^2 = 2.165\\) where\n\\(\\ln y = 4\\), i.e.\\ \\(y = e^4\\), \\(n = e^{e^4} - 16 \\approx 10^{23.7}\\),\nand returns below \\(1\\) only where \\((\\ln y)^2 = \\sqrt{y}\\) again, at\n\\(y = 5503.7\\), i.e.\\ \\(n \\approx e^{5504} \\approx 10^{2390}\\).\nTable~\\ref{tab:runtime-scale} gives the values and\nFigures~\\ref{fig:central} and \\ref{fig:runtime-scale} draw them; every\nnumber is printed by \\repofile{paper3/scripts/runtime\\_scale.py}\n(run under \\texttt{python3 -I}; it imports nothing from the clone and\ncomputes in \\(y\\), so that no \\(n\\) beyond floating-point range is ever\nformed). The crossing at 50 is a fact about integers:\n\\(\\rho(49)/49 = 0.9994\\) and \\(\\rho(50)/50 = 1.0027\\). In the limit\n\\(\\rhon/n \\to 0\\), since \\((\\ln y)^2/\\sqrt{y} \\to 0\\): the exponent is\n\\(o(n)\\), and that is a theorem in our companion Lean\n\\leanref{rho-little-o}. Both facts are true at once, and the second is\nthe one the title is about: the function is \\(o(n)\\), and it is above\n\\(n\\) on every size anyone will ever write down.\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\caption{The verified exponent relative to \\(n\\). \\(\\rhon = n(\\ln\\ln(n+16))^2/\\sqrt{\\ln(n+16)}\\),\nthe comparator's \\texttt{runtimeScale} (lines 132--134), natural logarithms.\nThe verified bound is \\(C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}\\); at \\(A = 1\\) its exponential\nfactor is \\(2^{(\\rhon/n)\\cdot n}\\), against \\(2^n\\) for trying every assignment.\nRegenerate: \\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/runtime\\_scale.py}.}\n\\label{tab:runtime-scale}\n\\input{tables/runtime_scale}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{figure}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{rho_over_n.pdf}\\\\[4pt]\n\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{rho_over_n_practical.pdf}\n\\caption{The verified exponent against brute force. Top: \\(\\rhon/n\\) over\nthe whole range, the axis in \\(\\log_{10} n\\) from \\(n = 10\\) to\n\\(n = 10^{3000}\\); the dashed level is \\(2^n\\); beneath it the manuscript's two\nsharper exponents, \\(n\\ln\\ln n/\\sqrt{\\ln n}\\) (Theorem~1.1, general) and\n\\(n/\\sqrt{\\ln n}\\) (fixed clause density), which are not in the Lean\nlibrary (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}). Bottom: the same three curves over the\npractical range \\(n = 10\\) to \\(10^6\\), with \\(\\rhon/n\\) marked at\n\\(n = 50, 125, 10^3, 10^4, 10^6\\). All curves at the constant \\(A = 1\\);\nthe verified \\(A\\) is unknown. Regenerate:\n\\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/rho\\_over\\_n.py}.}\n\\label{fig:central}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{figure}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{runtime_scale.pdf}\n\\caption{\\(\\rhon/n\\) alone, on a logarithmic axis in \\(\\log_{10} n\\) from\n\\(n = 10\\) to \\(n = 10^{2500}\\), with the level \\(1\\) (brute force, \\(2^n\\)),\nthe first integer above the level (\\(n = 50\\)), the peak (\\(16/e^2 = 2.17\\)\nat \\(\\ln(n+16) = e^4\\), \\(n \\approx 10^{23.7}\\)), the return below the level\n(\\(n \\approx e^{5504} \\approx 10^{2390}\\)), and the practical range\n\\(50 \\le n \\le 10^6\\) shaded. Regenerate:\n\\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/runtime\\_scale.py}.}\n\\label{fig:runtime-scale}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\subsection{The title's claim, made precise}\n\nThe title says the verified algorithm is slower than trying all \\(2^n\\)\nassignments at every practical size. Read against the statement, that is\nthe following, and nothing more.\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item \\textbf{What is verified is a guarantee, not a running time.} The\n  theorem says the machine halts within \\(C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}\\) steps.\n  It does not say how many steps the machine takes on any input, and no\n  implementation of the machine exists to be timed. A bound above\n  \\(2^n\\) does not say the machine is slow; it says the proof promises\n  nothing better than \\(2^n\\) there.\n\\item \\textbf{The constants \\(C, A, S, d, e\\) are existential and never\n  computed.} The comparator asserts their existence; the proof builds\n  them from existential lemmas (above, with \\(A \\ge 257\\) as\n  constructed) and states no value. The machine's state count is likewise\n  never computed.\n\\item \\textbf{With \\(A = 1\\), the verified exponent exceeds \\(n\\) for\n  every \\(50 \\le n < e^{5504}\\).} That is Table~\\ref{tab:runtime-scale}:\n  the exponential factor of the bound is at least \\(2^n\\) from 50\n  variables up to about \\(10^{2390}\\), and \\(2^{2.17\\,n}\\) at its\n  worst. With the true \\(A\\), whatever it is, the factor is\n  \\(2^{A\\rhon} \\ge 2^{\\rhon}\\) once \\(A \\ge 1\\), and the proof's own\n  construction has \\(A \\ge 257\\); so nothing is known that would put the\n  bound below \\(2^n\\) at any practical size.\n\\item \\textbf{Hence, at every practical size, the verified guarantee is\n  weaker than the guarantee of trying all assignments.} Trying all \\(2^n\\)\n  assignments and checking each against the \\(m\\) clauses counts the\n  models in \\(2^n \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(n+m)\\) steps on any reasonable\n  machine, with constants one can write down. The verified guarantee is\n  \\(C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}\\) with \\(\\rhon > n\\) on \\(50 \\le n < e^{5504}\\)\n  and \\(C, A, d\\) unknown. For every \\(C \\ge 1\\), \\(A \\ge 1\\) and\n  \\(d \\ge 1\\) the verified bound is above \\(2^n\\) on every \\(n\\) in that\n  range, and the only values on record are the proof's, which has\n  \\(1 \\le C\\) and \\(1 \\le A\\) in the large-input run's own statement\n  (\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLargeEntry.lean}, line 12) and\n  \\(A \\ge 257\\) in its construction. A proof with \\(A < 1\\) would be a\n  different proof; none is on record.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\nNone of this is a defect of the formalisation or of the proof; it is\nwhat an \\(O_k(1)\\) statement with a \\(2^{o(n)}\\) exponent of this shape\nmeans when the exponent's constants are not tracked. The manuscript\nitself calls \\(\\rhon\\) ``the weaker exponent'' (p.~2, line~63), and its\nsharper exponents are not in the Lean library (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}).\nOne more detail of base: the solution's internal \\(\\rho\\)\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Asymptotics.lean}, lines 10--11) is in base 2 and\nis related to \\texttt{runtimeScale} by\n\\(\\mathtt{runtimeScale}\\ n \\le 4\\,\\rho_2(n)\\) for \\(n \\ge 20\\)\n(lines 189--190); the base changes the exponent by a constant factor\nthat \\(A\\) absorbs, so nothing above depends on it.\n\n\\subsection{What the statement does establish}\n\nFor each fixed \\(k \\ge 2\\), a deterministic machine that, on the canonical\nencoding of any \\(k\\)-CNF on \\(n\\) variables with \\(m\\) clauses, halts with\nthe exact number of satisfying assignments in binary on a named tape,\nwithin \\(C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}\\) steps, where \\(L = O(k\\,n\\,m)\\) is\npolynomial in \\(n + m\\) and \\(\\rhon = o(n)\\); and, because a\n\\texttt{Finset} of clauses of width at most \\(k\\) has at most\n\\(\\sum_{j \\le k}\\binom{2n}{j} = O(n^k)\\) members (\\(2{,}604{,}376\\) at\n\\(n = 125\\), \\(k = 3\\)), the whole bound is \\(2^{o(n)}\\) in \\(n\\) alone.\nThat is a deterministic \\(2^{o(n)} \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(n+m)\\) algorithm\nfor \\#\\(k\\)-SAT in the standard sense, and therefore for \\(k\\)-SAT; it\nrefutes the Exponential Time Hypothesis \\citep{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001} as\nstated. The statement reads as the manuscript's verified form, the\nweaker exponent of its Proposition~6.4 (p.~43, lines 2474--2476), without\nthe \\(n \\ge 20\\) proviso. We found no field, definition or quantifier\nthrough which the statement could be kernel-true and mean something\nweaker than this; the audit it summarises is\n\\repofile{reports/scratch/eth_audit/statement\\_audit.md}. Our own\nrestatement of the statement as a Lean \\texttt{Prop}, character for\ncharacter over a verbatim copy of the machine model, is\n\\leanref{subexpcount}; it is never proved and never an axiom there\n(Section~\\ref{sec:lean}).\n\n\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{No running time of any machine.} Nothing here measures or\n  estimates how long the verified machine, or any program, takes on any\n  input. The title compares two \\emph{guarantees}; Section~\\ref{sec:practitioner}\n  is the only place in the paper where anything is timed, and it times\n  ordinary solvers, not this machine.\n\\item \\textbf{No statement about the proof.} That the statement means\n  what we say it means is a reading of 220 lines; whether the proof of\n  it is sound is Section~\\ref{sec:proof} and Section~\\ref{sec:replay},\n  which found no flaw. The one proof-side fact quoted here, \\(A = 256 +\n  A_t\\), is a line of the solution read for the value it assigns, not a\n  claim about the argument.\n\\item \\textbf{No value of the constants.} \\(A \\ge 257\\) is what the\n  proof writes down, not what the machine needs; a different proof of\n  the same statement could have any \\(A > 0\\). The statement itself\n  fixes nothing.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about the manuscript's sharper exponents}, which\n  are not in the Lean library and are Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}'s subject.\n\\item \\textbf{No claim about sizes beyond \\(e^{5504}\\).} Past\n  \\(n \\approx 10^{2390}\\) the verified factor is below \\(2^n\\) at\n  \\(A = 1\\); we say nothing about that range except that it is not a\n  practical one.\n\\end{itemize}\n======\n# ETH_AMBIGUOUS — questions the loop decided for itself, for the owner\n\n**Protocol** (owner, 2026-11-05). Paper 3 is written by Ralph loop0015\nwith no stopping midway. Whenever an item finds something ambiguous — a\nwording, a claim, a citation form, a choice between two sound ways of\ndoing a thing — it **decides**, writes **one entry here per question**,\nand continues. Each entry has three parts: *what was ambiguous*, *what\nthe item chose*, *what the owner may want to change*. Entries are dated\nand numbered (`S` for setup, then the item number and a letter: `01a`,\n`01b`, …). Nothing here changes the title, which is fixed:\n\n> Exponential time hypothesis is false... but the verified algorithm is slower than trying all 2^n assignments at every practical size.\n\nItem 09 lists in the paper's conclusion the entries that affect the text;\nitem 11 lists all of them in `README.md`. The owner resolves an entry by\nediting the paper and striking the entry (or by leaving it, which is also\nan answer).\n\n## Entries\n\n### S1 — the author line (setup, 2026-11-05)\n- **Ambiguous**: who the authors are. Paper 1 is Linhares and Yanasse;\n  the brief for this paper names \"Alexandre Linhares\" and \"the MOSP\n  project\" as a placeholder.\n- **Chosen**: `\\author{Alexandre Linhares \\and the MOSP project}` in\n  `latex/main.tex`, with a comment marking the second author as a\n  placeholder; the acknowledgements will carry the AI-use disclosure in\n  paper 1's form.\n- **Owner may change**: the second author (Yanasse? none? a project\n  name?), affiliations, ORCID.\n\n### S2 — `2^n` in the typeset title (setup, 2026-11-05)\n- **Ambiguous**: the title string carries the plain-text `2^n`; LaTeX\n  cannot typeset `^` in text mode, and a typeset title would ordinarily\n  show a superscript.\n- **Chosen**: `\\title{... all $2^n$ assignments ...}` (a superscript `n`\n  in the PDF) with `\\ldots` for the ellipsis; the PDF metadata\n  (`pdftitle`) carries the plain string with the caret literal (`2\\string^n`, since hyperref drops a bare `^` from PDF strings); the plain\n  string also sits in a comment directly above `\\title`. The gate strips\n  `$` and `\\ldots` before comparing. Nothing else in the title is\n  touched.\n- **Owner may change**: typeset the caret literally (`2\\textasciicircum\n  n`) if the plain form is wanted on the page.\n\n### S3 — where the companion Lean lives (setup, 2026-11-05)\n- **Ambiguous**: the restatement `SubexpCount` and the verbatim machine\n  model are under `lean/MOSPFormalization/SubexpPathwidth/`, a path\n  named for loop0014's pathwidth question, which this paper does not\n  touch. Cite them there, or move them to a neutral path?\n- **Chosen**: item 07 moves them — `PrimitiveMachine.lean` and the\n  ETH-only declarations of `Frame.lean` (`SubexpCount`,\n  `ExactCountAndSat`, `SubexpCount.anti`, `ExactCountAndSat.subexpCount`,\n  `runtimeBound_eq`, `tendsto_log_sq_div_sqrt`, `runtimeScale_isLittleO`)\n  — to `lean/MOSPFormalization/ETH/{PrimitiveMachine,Statement}.lean`;\n  `SubexpPathwidth/Frame.lean` imports `ETH.Statement` and keeps its\n  pathwidth side. loop0014's report will then name the old path for\n  those declarations; a one-line note in `reports/subexp_pathwidth.md`\n  §2.1 is the smallest fix and item 07 adds it.\n- **Owner may change**: keep them in place and cite the old path; or a\n  different directory name.\n\n### S4 — Bonnet's venue year, and the width references dropped (setup, 2026-11-05)\n- **Ambiguous**: the original brief listed Lokshtanov–Marx–Saurabh,\n  Cygan et al. (Cut & Count), Lampis 2025 and Bonnet 2025 for `refs.bib`;\n  the owner's scope change the same day removed every width-parameter\n  consequence from the paper, so none of the four is cited. (For the\n  record, Crossref on 2026-11-05 gives Bonnet as STOC 2025,\n  `10.1145/3717823.3718117`, and *J. ACM* issued 2026-10-10,\n  `10.1145/3833387`; loop0014's report says \"*J. ACM* 2025\".)\n- **Chosen**: the four entries are not in `refs.bib`; `refs.bib` carries\n  the OpenAI manuscript, Impagliazzo–Paturi 2001, Impagliazzo–Paturi–Zane\n  2001, the human-audit paper arXiv 2608.14673, and `@misc` entries for\n  the comparator and nanoda repositories (URLs; item 08 fills what it\n  fetches).\n- **Owner may change**: nothing unless a width reference is wanted back.\n\n### S5 — per-item cap in the driver (setup, 2026-11-05)\n- **Ambiguous**: the loop0014 driver has one global c