or each fixed q ⩾ 2, the number of proper q-colorings can be computed deterministically in 2o(v) time\np2:102: and polyq (v, e) space. The number of perfect matchings can be computed deterministically in 2o(v+e)\np2:103: time and poly(v, e) space.\np2:104: \f                           THE EXPONENTIAL TIME HYPOTHESIS IS FALSE                                 3\np3:105: \np3:106: \np3:107: Proof. For G = (V, E), introduce a Boolean variable xu for each u ∈ V . Satisfying assignments of\np3:108:                                         ^\np3:109:                                              (¬xu ∨ ¬xw )\np3:110:                                         {u,w}∈E\np3:111: \np3:112: correspond bijectively to the independent sets {u ∈ V : xu = 1}. For proper q-colorings, use one\np3:113: Boolean variable per vertex and color, require exactly one color at each vertex, and forbid equal\np3:114: colors at adjacent vertices. This gives a counting-preserving q-CNF encoding with qv variables and\np3:115: Oq (v + e) clauses.\np3:116:    For perfect matchings, use one Boolean variable per edge and a linear-size circuit checking that\np3:117: exactly one selected edge meets each vertex. The standard circuit-to-3-CNF reduction [Bar23,\np3:118: Lemmas 15.9–15.10] gives O(v + e) variables and clauses. The gate variables have unique values\np3:119: once the edge bits are fixed, so this encoding also preserves the count. Theorem 1.1 gives the stated\np3:120: time and space bounds.                                                                             □\np3:121:   The fixed-density case of Theorem 1.1 gives a circuit lower bound for ENP , the class of languages\np3:122: decidable in deterministic time 2O(n) with an NP oracle.\np3:123: Corollary 1.4. Let CL (n) be the minimum number of noninput gates in a circuit computing L on\np3:124: {0, 1}n , allowing arbitrary Boolean gates of fan-in at most two and unrestricted fan-out. There exist\np3:125: a language L ∈ ENP and a constant c > 0 such that\np3:126:                                          p\np3:127:                              CL (n) > c n log n      for infinitely many n.                      (1.2)\np3:128:   Appendix C.1 proves the corollary. Appendix C.2 gives algorithms for inversion and exact preimage\np3:129: counting of Boolean maps with constant output locality.\np3:130: 1.1. Related work. We first discuss earlier SAT and counting algorithms and the complexity\np3:131: hypotheses contradicted by Theorem 1.1. We then compare our approach with earlier algebraic\np3:132: algorithms and identify the existing constructions used in our proof, whose roles are developed in\np3:133: the roadmap in Section 2.\np3:134: 1.1.1. SAT algorithms and the exponential-time hypotheses. Schöning’s local-search algorithm and\np3:135: PPSZ are landmarks in the development of exponential-time algorithms for k-SAT [Sch99, PPSZ05].","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01EaUK63ai74Gqwz9NGEjiF3","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
