{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012R8WVdWwdHVFjr5SDhXNoz","type":"tool_result","content":"p3:134: 1.1.1. SAT algorithms and the exponential-time hypotheses. Schöning’s local-search algorithm and\np3:135: PPSZ are landmarks in the development of exponential-time algorithms for k-SAT [Sch99, PPSZ05].\np3:136: Schöning’s randomized algorithm starts from a random assignment and repeatedly flips a uniformly\np3:137: chosen variable in an unsatisfied clause. Its bounded-error decision time is\np3:138:                                        (n + m)O(1) (2 − 2/k)n\np3:139: for each fixed k ⩾ 3 [Sch99, Section 2]. In particular, this gives (n + m)O(1) (4/3)n time for 3-SAT.\np3:140: The PPSZ algorithm [PPSZ05] uses a different mechanism: bounded resolution derives additional\np3:141: clauses, after which variables are assigned in a random order, taking forced values when available and\np3:142: guessing otherwise. For k = 3, 4, its original analysis gave stronger bounds for uniquely satisfiable\np3:143: formulas than for general ones. The unique-SAT guarantees were later extended to general 3-SAT\np3:144: and 4-SAT; for 3-SAT this gave a randomized O(1.308n ) bound [Her14]. Hansen, Kaplan, Zamir, and\np3:145: Zwick improved the PPSZ bound for every fixed k ⩾ 3 by biasing the algorithm’s guesses [HKZZ19].\np3:146: Scheder obtained an improvement for the same range of k by sharpening the analysis of the original\np3:147: PPSZ algorithm [Sch24, Theorem 1.5]. For general 3-SAT, a preprint by Jiang and Cai gives a\np3:148: randomized O∗ (1.307031578n ) bound [JC26, Corollary 1.2], where O∗ suppresses factors polynomial\np3:149: in the input size. Liu’s deterministic algorithm combines branching with derandomized local search\np3:150: and solves 3-SAT in O(1.32793n ) time [Liu18, Theorem 21].\np3:151:    Randomized local search has also produced effective heuristics. The WalkSAT heuristic chooses an\np3:152: unsatisfied clause and flips one of its variables, combining random choices with greedy moves [SKC94].\np3:153: Experiments on formulas from circuit synthesis and diagnosis demonstrated the practical value of\np3:154: this approach [SKC94, pp. 340–342, Tables 4–5].\np3:155: \f4                                                OPENAI\np4:156: \np4:157: \np4:158:    Among uniformly random k-CNFs, instances at the satisfiability threshold were conjectured\np4:159: to be the hardest for deciding satisfiability [VW21, Section 1]; for fixed k ⩾ 3 and lower clause\np4:160: densities m/n ⩽ 2k /k C , where C is a universal constant, polynomial-time almost-uniform sampling\np4:161: of satisfying assignments is known with high probability over the formula [CLWYY25, Theorem 1.2].\np4:162: For a uniformly random formula with polynomially many clauses, conditioned on satisfiability, Vyas\np4:163: and Williams [VW21, Theorem 3] give a PPZ-based algorithm that finds a solution in 2n(1−Ω(log k/k))\np4:164: time with high probability over both the formula and the algorithm’s random choices.\np4:165:    The Exponential Time Hypothesis (ETH) asserted that 3-SAT requires 2Ω(n) deterministic time\np4:166: in the worst case, and hence so does k-SAT for every fixed k ⩾ 3 [IP01, Theorem 1]. The Strong\np4:167: Exponential Time Hypothesis (SETH) made the sharper prediction that k-SAT requires 2n(1−ok (1))\np4:168: time as k grows. Precisely, for every ε ∈ (0, 1), there is a fixed k for which no deterministic algorithm\np4:169: solves k-SAT in 2(1−ε)n (n + m)O(1) time. The running-time bounds for Schöning’s algorithm and\np4:170: PPSZ helped motivate this prediction: their improvements over exhaustive search diminish as k\np4:171: grows [IP01, Open Problems, item 2].\np4:172:    The Gap Exponential Time Hypothesis (Gap-ETH) strengthens ETH by postulating constants\np4:173: δ, γ > 0 for which no O(2γn )-time algorithm can distinguish a satisfiable 3-CNF from one in which\np4:174: every assignment violates at least a δ fraction of the clauses, even when m = O(n) [Din16, MR17].\np4:175: The randomized version rules out an algorithm with the same running time that distinguishes\np4:176: the two cases with probability at least 2/3 on every promised input [CCK+ 20, Conjecture 2.5].\np4:177: It has been used to prove hardness of approximating Label Cover [Din16] and dense constraint\np4:178: satisfaction problems [MR17]. It also rules out nontrivial fixed-parameter approximations for Clique\np4:179: and Dominating Set. For Clique, the excluded ratios are o(OPT), where OPT is the maximum\np4:180: clique size [CCK+ 20, Corollaries 5.3 and 5.6].\np4:181:    The Nondeterministic Strong Exponential Time Hypothesis (NSETH) asserts that, for every ε > 0,\np4:182: some fixed k admits no nondeterministic 2(1−ε)n (n + m)O(1) -time algorithm for deciding whether\np4:183: a k-CNF is unsatisfiable, or equivalently whether a k-DNF is a tautology [CGI+ 16]. NSETH has\np4:184: been used to establish barriers to deterministic fine-grained reductions from SAT to problems such\np4:185: as 3SUM and All-Pairs Shortest Paths [CGI+ 16], and to delineate SETH-based lower bounds for\np4:186: approximating directed graph diameter [Li21].\np4:187:    Theorem 1.1 refutes ETH, SETH, Gap-ETH, and NSETH. Indeed, testing whether the exact count\np4:188: is positive or zero decides both satisfiability and unsatisfiability in deterministic 2o(n) (n+m)Ok (1) time\np4:189: for every fixed k; deciding satisfiability also solves the Gap-ETH promise problem. The deterministic\np4:190: algorithm also refutes the bounded-error randomized versions of ETH [DHM+ 14, Section 1] and\np4:191: SETH [CDL+ 16, Definition 2.1], and of Gap-ETH. It does not conflict with unconditional exponential\np4:192: lower bounds for restricted proof systems such as regular resolution [BI13]. In 2016, Ryan Williams\np4:193: refuted the Merlin–Arthur version of SETH (MA-SETH) by giving a protocol that certifies k-CNF\np4:194: unsatisfiability in 2n/2 poly(n, m) time [Wil16, Theorem 3.3].\np4:195: \np4:196: \np4:197: 1.1.2. Exact counting and counting complexity. In Valiant’s framework, #SAT and #3-SAT are\np4:198: #P-complete under parsimonious reductions [Val79, Section 3, Facts 1–2]. For fixed k ⩾ 3, previous\np4:199: exact-counting algorithms achieve polyk (n, m)2n−Ω(n/k) time, with a Las Vegas expected-time\np4:200: bound in [IMP12, Lemma 3.5] and a deterministic bound in [CW21, Theorem 1.6]. For #2-SAT,\np4:201: Wahlström’s algorithm counts satisfying assignments in O∗ (1.2377n ) time [Wah08]. For #3-SAT,\np4:202: Kutzkov uses complementary branching to count satisfying assignments in O∗ (1.637n ) time [Kut26].\np4:203: Both algorithms are deterministic.\np4:204:    The counting Exponential Time Hypothesis (#ETH) asserted that exactly counting the satisfying\np4:205: assignments of a 3-CNF requires 2Ω(n) deterministic time [DHM+ 14, Section 1]. Under #ETH,\np4:206: Curticapean [Cur18] rules out deterministic 2o(v) -time algorithms for counting perfect matchings\np4:207: even in unweighted bipartite graphs with v vertices and O(v) edges. The perfect-matching algorithm\np4:208: \f                            THE EXPONENTIAL TIME HYPOTHESIS IS FALSE                                   5\np5:209: \np5:210: \np5:211: in Corollary 1.3 applies in particular to these graphs. Our algorithm (Theorem 1.1) solves #3-SAT\np5:212: deterministically in 2o(n) poly(n, m) time and therefore refutes #ETH.\np5:213: 1.1.3. Implications for fine-grained complexity. ETH and SETH became standard assumptions for\np5:214: parameterized and fine-grained complexity [IPZ01, VW19]. ETH-based reductions yielded lower\np5:215: bounds for the dependence on solution size in Clique [CHKX06], and on treewidth in constraint\np5:216: satisfaction and partitioned subgraph isomorphism [Mar10]. SETH supported sharp exponential\np5:217: bounds for Hitting Set and Set Splitting [CDL+ 16], and for Independent Set and Dominating Set\np5:218: parameterized by treewidth [LMS18]. It also gave bounds on possible speedups of pseudopolynomial\np5:219: algorithms for Subset Sum and Bicriteria Path [ABHS19].\np5:220:    For problems in P, SETH was used to rule out polynomial-factor speedups over quadratic running\np5:221: times for edit distance [BI18], longest common subsequence and dynamic time warping [BK15],\np5:222: Fréchet distance [Bri14], and regular-expression matching [BI16]. Other applications concerned\np5:223: approximating graph diameter [RV13] and maintaining reachability counts or the size of the largest\np5:224: strongly connected component under graph updates [AW14]. Together these results made SETH a\np5:225: common basis for explaining the apparent optimality of algorithms across quite different problems.\np5:226:    Our refutation of ETH and SETH removes one basis for the hardness claims above. Some\np5:227: of the same lower bounds follow from weaker hypotheses that may still hold. For example, the\np5:228: lower bounds for longest common subsequence and regular-expression matching follow from the\np5:229: Orthogonal Vectors Conjecture (OVC) [BK15, BI16]. OVC asserts that for every fixed ε > 0, no\np5:230: O(N 2−ε poly(d))-time algorithm can decide whether two sets of N Boolean vectors of dimension\np5:231: d contain an orthogonal pair, one vector from each set. The reduction from SAT to Orthogonal\np5:232: Vectors shows that SETH implies OVC [Wil05, VW19]. The lower bounds for Fréchet distance,\np5:233: graph diameter approximation, and the treewidth dependence of Independent Set and Dominating\np5:234: Set also follow from the hypothesis that unrestricted CNF-SAT admits no 2(1−δ)n poly(n, m)-time\np5:235: algorithm for any constant δ > 0 [Bri14, RV13, LMS18]. Theorem 1.1 gives a running-time guarantee\np5:236: for each fixed clause width k, while this hypothesis allows the width to grow with n.\np5:237:    To obtain a faster algorithm for a target problem from our SAT algorithm, we need a reduction in\np5:238: the other direction, from that problem to fixed-width SAT, that controls the number of variables.\np5:239: Set Splitting with sets of size at most k has such a reduction [CDL+ 16, Observation 3.6]: use one\np5:240: Boolean variable per element to specify its side of the split. For each set, one clause requires at least\np5:241: one of its variables to be true, and a second requires at least one to be false. The resulting k-CNF\np5:242: has one variable per universe element, so for fixed k Theorem 1.1 gives subexponential time in the\np5:243: universe size, up to a polynomial input-size factor.\np5:244: 1.1.4. Prior work on algebraic algorithms for combinatorial problems. Our algorithm is algebraic,\np5:245: based on polynomial interpolation of carefully chosen auxiliary variables. Below we discuss prior work\np5:246: on using algebraic methods to design algorithm for combinatorial problems. For broader background,\np5:247: see the surveys of Koutis and Williams [KW16] on algebraic fingerprints, Williams [Wil14] on the\np5:248: polynomial method in algorithm design, and Husfeldt [Hus11] on algorithmic inclusion–exclusion.\np5:249:    Williams’s split-and-list algorithm [Wil05] partitions the variables of a 2-CSP into three groups,\np5:250: enumerates partial assignments, and combines them using fast matrix multiplication. Koivisto [Koi06]\np5:251: gives a direct sum–product formulation. The algorithm of Sections 3–6 likewise enumerates as-\np5:252: signments within groups. Fixing the shared measurements ⃗y makes their contributions multiply\np5:253: independently. Scott and Sorkin [SS09, Section 6] extend split-and-list to polynomial-valued weights\np5:254: and arbitrary rings. Our summation routine similarly uses truncated polynomial rings to retain\np5:255: several coefficients in one computation.\np5:256:    Björklund [Bjö16, Sections 2–3] uses inclusion–exclusion and polynomial interpolation to reduce\np5:257: permanent and directed Hamiltonian-cycle counting to a weighted sum over smaller instances. He\np5:258: adds the weights of identical instances in a table, then solves each distinct instance with nonzero\np5:259: total weight once. Björklund, Kaski, and Williams [BKW19] use low-degree curves and generalized\np5:260: \f6                                              OPENAI\np6:261: \np6:262: \np6:263: Kakeya sets to recover polynomial values from evaluations on a small structured set, yielding faster\np6:264: algorithms for fermionants, including the permanent of integer matrices whose entries are bounded\np6:265: in absolute value by a fixed constant. Our polynomial replacements serve a similar purpose: they\np6:266: connect the desired count to structured evaluations from which it can be reconstructed. Here the\np6:267: useful structure is separation into groups after fixing a few shared measurements.\np6:268:    Bhargava, Ghosh, Kumar, and Mohapatra [BGKM23, Sections 3.1–3.2] evaluate multivariate\np6:269: polynomials over finite fields along low-degree curves, using Hasse derivatives and Hermite interpola-\np6:270: tion when values alone do not suffice. For a univariate restriction g, the order-j Hasse derivative at\np6:271: t is the coefficient of z j in g(t + z). We use the same recovery principle: compute several Taylor\np6:272: coefficients at each point admitting fast summation, then recover the original count by Hermite\np6:273: interpolation.\np6:274:    Woodruff and Yekhanin [WY07] use values and first derivatives along a line to recover a target\np6:275: value in private information retrieval. Kopparty, Saraf, and Yekhanin [KSY14] use higher-order\np6:276: Hasse derivatives for local correction of multiplicity codes, extending the line-based recovery of\np6:277: Reed–Muller codes. In our algorithm, the matrix construction supplies low-degree replacement forms\np6:278: that let us compute the Taylor coefficients at the interpolation points by fast summation after fixing\np6:279: a few shared measurements.\np6:280: 1.1.5. Antecedents of the matrix construction. Our counting algorithm evaluates sums of products\np6:281: of polynomials over Boolean assignments. The polynomials share variables, so these sums do not\np6:282: factor into independent smaller sums. We introduce linear substitutions, depending polynomially on\np6:283: an interpolation parameter, that make such a factorization possible after fixing a few integer linear\np6:284: measurements. An auxiliary square rational matrix M0 underlies these substitutions: a bound on\np6:285: rank(I − M0 ) limits the number of measurements, while restrictions on the nonzero entries of M0\np6:286: keep the substitution degrees small. Section 2.7 explains this connection.\np6:287:    Related interpolation methods have been used to obtain low-rank approximations to structured\np6:288: matrices. In [AW17, Section 3], a low-degree polynomial matches (−1)j on an interval of integer\np6:289: inner products j, using the interpolation lemma of [AW15, Lemma 3.1]. This gives low-rank\np6:290: matrices agreeing with Walsh–Hadamard matrices in most entries, a result in the study of matrix\np6:291: rigidity [Val77]. Related interpolation gives rigidity bounds for Kronecker powers in [AGP23,\np6:292: Theorem 6.1]. Dvir and Liu [DL20, Claim 2.20 and Lemmas 3.3–3.4] use a spectral approach: sparse\np6:293: changes to a function invariant under permutations of its coordinates make many Fourier coefficients\np6:294: vanish, reducing the rank of its group-circulant matrix.\np6:295:    Our construction also interpolates (−1)j on a short interval, now to obtain a polynomial in\np6:296: commuting permutation matrices. The same polynomial gives both the rank bound for I − M0\np6:297: and the restrictions on the nonzero entries of M0 . We prove these properties and their quantitative\np6:298: bounds directly in Section 4.\np6:299: 1.1.6. Sparsification and parity tests. We use the counting-preserving version [DHM+ 14, Appendix A]\np6:300: of the sparsification lemma [IPZ01], with quantitative bounds from [CIP06, Section 6] and [Cal09,\np6:301: Lemma 5.14 and equation (5.4)]. This partitions the satisfying assignments among sparse formulas\np6:302: on the same Boolean variables. Appendix A records the disjoint branching argument and the bounds\np6:303: needed when its parameter varies with the input size.\np6:304:    The initial parity tests are the constraints of a Tanner code [Tan81, Section II], applied to the\np6:305: binary vector recording which clauses an assignment violates. We use the edge-based expander-code\np6:306: construction of [SS96, Sections IV and VI]: each graph edge carries one bit, and each vertex requires\np6:307: its incident bits to belong to a fixed binary linear code. The local code’s minimum distance, together\np6:308: with graph expansion, forces every nonzero vector satisfying all these constraints to have large\np6:309: support. This construction and distance argument are standard. Lemma 6.1 supplies the parameter\np6:310: choices needed here, including the bounds on the number and support size of the tests, and constructs\np6:311: the graph and local code by deterministic exhaustive searches. The graph is the bipartite double\np6:312: \f                               THE EXPONENTIAL TIME HYPOTHESIS IS FALSE                                          7\np7:313: \np7:314: \np7:315: cover of a Cayley multigraph. The shifts defining that multigraph nearly balance every nonconstant\np7:316: parity on the base group: this is the small-bias condition [NN93, AR94], stated explicitly in (6.2).\np7:317: Fixing unused edge bits to zero gives a code on exactly m coordinates. Additional parity tests\np7:318: exclude the remaining nonzero violation vectors. We choose these tests by the averaging principle\np7:319: underlying the method of conditional expectations [Rag88], using exact counts to evaluate each\np7:320: candidate. Section 6 gives the constructions and quantitative bounds needed here.\np7:321:    Theorem 1.1 invites a renewed search for algorithms for problems whose known hardness results\np7:322: relied on ETH or SETH. The examples in Section 1.1.3 suggest both immediate consequences and\np7:323: problems for which new algorithmic ideas are needed.\np7:324: \np7:325: 1.2. Notation. For an integer s ⩾ 0, write [s] = {1, . . . , s}, with [0] = ∅. For a condition E,\np7:326: the scalar 1{E} equals one if E holds and zero otherwise. We use natural logarithms, writing\np7:327: log2 when the base is two. For complexity bounds, poly(t1 , . . . , ts ) denotes a bound of the form\np7:328: (1+t1 +· · ·+ts )O(1) , with absolute implicit constants. Subscripts, as in polyk,c (t1 , . . . , ts ), allow those\np7:329: constants to depend on the indicated fixed parameters. Integers are stored in signed binary notation,\np7:330: so an integer z uses O(log(|z| + 2)) bits. Rational numbers are stored as an integer numerator and a","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"43cbaf52-3109-48ae-9853-92f13356d98c","uuid":"ae9a0674-54da-4ce7-83fe-b2cb45b7d914","timestamp":"2026-11-05T02:50:41.336Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"p3:134: 1.1.1. SAT algorithms and the exponential-time hypotheses. Schöning’s local-search algorithm and\np3:135: PPSZ are landmarks in the development of exponential-time algorithms for k-SAT [Sch99, PPSZ05].\np3:136: Schöning’s randomized algorithm starts from a random assignment and repeatedly flips a uniformly\np3:137: chosen variable in an unsatisfied clause. Its bounded-error decision time is\np3:138:                                        (n + m)O(1) (2 − 2/k)n\np3:139: for each fixed k ⩾ 3 [Sch99, Section 2]. In particular, this gives (n + m)O(1) (4/3)n time for 3-SAT.\np3:140: The PPSZ algorithm [PPSZ05] uses a different mechanism: bounded resolution derives additional\np3:141: clauses, after which variables are assigned in a random order, taking forced values when available and\np3:142: guessing otherwise. For k = 3, 4, its original analysis gave stronger bounds for uniquely satisfiable\np3:143: formulas than for general ones. The unique-SAT guarantees were later extended to general 3-SAT\np3:144: and 4-SAT; for 3-SAT this gave a randomized O(1.308n ) bound [Her14]. Hansen, Kaplan, Zamir, and\np3:145: Zwick improved the PPSZ bound for every fixed k ⩾ 3 by biasing the algorithm’s guesses [HKZZ19].\np3:146: Scheder obtained an improvement for the same range of k by sharpening the analysis of the original\np3:147: PPSZ algorithm [Sch24, Theorem 1.5]. For general 3-SAT, a preprint by Jiang and Cai gives a\np3:148: randomized O∗ (1.307031578n ) bound [JC26, Corollary 1.2], where O∗ suppresses factors polynomial\np3:149: in the input size. Liu’s deterministic algorithm combines branching with derandomized local search\np3:150: and solves 3-SAT in O(1.32793n ) time [Liu18, Theorem 21].\np3:151:    Randomized local search has also produced effective heuristics. The WalkSAT heuristic chooses an\np3:152: unsatisfied clause and flips one of its variables, combining random choices with greedy moves [SKC94].\np3:153: Experiments on formulas from circuit synthesis and diagnosis demonstrated the practical value of\np3:154: this approach [SKC94, pp. 340–342, Tables 4–5].\np3:155: \f4                                                OPENAI\np4:156: \np4:157: \np4:158:    Among uniformly random k-CNFs, instances at the satisfiability threshold were conjectured\np4:159: to be the hardest for deciding satisfiability [VW21, Section 1]; for fixed k ⩾ 3 and lower clause\np4:160: densities m/n ⩽ 2k /k C , where C is a universal constant, polynomial-time almost-uniform sampling\np4:161: of satisfying assignments is known with high probability over the formula [CLWYY25, Theorem 1.2].\np4:162: For a uniformly random formula with polynomially many clauses, conditioned on satisfiability, Vyas\np4:163: and Williams [VW21, Theorem 3] give a PPZ-based algorithm that finds a solution in 2n(1−Ω(log k/k))\np4:164: time with high probability over both the formula and the algorithm’s random choices.\np4:165:    The Exponential Time Hypothesis (ETH) asserted that 3-SAT requires 2Ω(n) deterministic time\np4:166: in the worst case, and hence so does k-SAT for every fixed k ⩾ 3 [IP01, Theorem 1]. The Strong\np4:167: Exponential Time Hypothesis (SETH) made the sharper prediction that k-SAT requires 2n(1−ok (1))\np4:168: time as k grows. Precisely, for every ε ∈ (0, 1), there is a fixed k for which no deterministic algorithm\np4:169: solves k-SAT in 2(1−ε)n (n + m)O(1) time. The running-time bounds for Schöning’s algorithm and\np4:170: PPSZ helped motivate this prediction: their improvements over exhaustive search diminish as k\np4:171: grows [IP01, Open Problems, item 2].\np4:172:    The Gap Exponential Time Hypothesis (Gap-ETH) strengthens ETH by postulating constants\np4:173: δ, γ > 0 for which no O(2γn )-time algorithm can distinguish a satisfiable 3-CNF from one in which\np4:174: every assignment violates at least a δ fraction of the clauses, even when m = O(n) [Din16, MR17].\np4:175: The randomized version rules out an algorithm with the same running time that distinguishes\np4:176: the two cases with probability at least 2/3 on every promised input [CCK+ 20, Conjecture 2.5].\np4:177: It has been used to prove hardness of approximating Label Cover [Din16] and dense constraint\np4:178: satisfaction problems [MR17]. It also rules out nontrivial fixed-parameter approximations for Clique\np4:179: and Dominating Set. For Clique, the excluded ratios are o(OPT), where OPT is the maximum\np4:180: clique size [CCK+ 20, Corollaries 5.3 and 5.6].\np4:181:    The Nondeterministic Strong Exponential Time Hypothesis (NSETH) asserts that, for every ε > 0,\np4:182: some fixed k admits no nondeterministic 2(1−ε)n (n + m)O(1) -time algorithm for deciding whether\np4:183: a k-CNF is unsatisfiable, or equivalently whether a k-DNF is a tautology [CGI+ 16]. NSETH has\np4:184: been used to establish barriers to deterministic fine-grained reductions from SAT to problems such\np4:185: as 3SUM and All-Pairs Shortest Paths [CGI+ 16], and to delineate SETH-based lower bounds for\np4:186: approximating directed graph diameter [Li21].\np4:187:    Theorem 1.1 refutes ETH, SETH, Gap-ETH, and NSETH. Indeed, testing whether the exact count\np4:188: is positive or zero decides both satisfiability and unsatisfiability in deterministic 2o(n) (n+m)Ok (1) time\np4:189: for every fixed k; deciding satisfiability also solves the Gap-ETH promise problem. The deterministic\np4:190: algorithm also refutes the bounded-error randomized versions of ETH [DHM+ 14, Section 1] and\np4:191: SETH [CDL+ 16, Definition 2.1], and of Gap-ETH. It does not conflict with unconditional exponential\np4:192: lower bounds for restricted proof systems such as regular resolution [BI13]. In 2016, Ryan Williams\np4:193: refuted the Merlin–Arthur version of SETH (MA-SETH) by giving a protocol that certifies k-CNF\np4:194: unsatisfiability in 2n/2 poly(n, m) time [Wil16, Theorem 3.3].\np4:195: \np4:196: \np4:197: 1.1.2. Exact counting and counting complexity. In Valiant’s framework, #SAT and #3-SAT are\np4:198: #P-complete under parsimonious reductions [Val79, Section 3, Facts 1–2]. For fixed k ⩾ 3, previous\np4:199: exact-counting algorithms achieve polyk (n, m)2n−Ω(n/k) time, with a Las Vegas expected-time\np4:200: bound in [IMP12, Lemma 3.5] and a deterministic bound in [CW21, Theorem 1.6]. For #2-SAT,\np4:201: Wahlström’s algorithm counts satisfying assignments in O∗ (1.2377n ) time [Wah08]. For #3-SAT,\np4:202: Kutzkov uses complementary branching to count satisfying assignments in O∗ (1.637n ) time [Kut26].\np4:203: Both algorithms are deterministic.\np4:204:    The counting Exponential Time Hypothesis (#ETH) asserted that exactly counting the satisfying\np4:205: assignments of a 3-CNF requires 2Ω(n) deterministic time [DHM+ 14, Section 1]. Under #ETH,\np4:206: Curticapean [Cur18] rules out deterministic 2o(v) -time algorithms for counting perfect matchings\np4:207: even in unweighted bipartite graphs with v vertices and O(v) edges. The perfect-matching algorithm\np4:208: \f                            THE EXPONENTIAL TIME HYPOTHESIS IS FALSE                                   5\np5:209: \np5:210: \np5:211: in Corollary 1.3 applies in particular to these graphs. Our algorithm (Theorem 1.1) solves #3-SAT\np5:212: deterministically in 2o(n) poly(n, m) time and therefore refutes #ETH.\np5:213: 1.1.3. Implications for fine-grained complexity. ETH and SETH became standard assumptions for\np5:214: parameterized and fine-grained complexity [IPZ01, VW19]. ETH-based reductions yielded lower\np5:215: bounds for the dependence on solution size in Clique [CHKX06], and on treewidth in constraint\np5:216: satisfaction and partitioned subgraph isomorphism [Mar10]. SETH supported sharp exponential\np5:217: bounds for Hitting Set and Set Splitting [CDL+ 16], and for Independent Set and Dominating Set\np5:218: parameterized by treewidth [LMS18]. It also gave bounds on possible speedups of pseudopolynomial\np5:219: algorithms for Subset Sum and Bicriteria Path [ABHS19].\np5:220:    For problems in P, SETH was used to rule out polynomial-factor speedups over quadratic running\np5:221: times for edit distance [BI18], longest common subsequence and dynamic time warping [BK15],\np5:222: Fréchet distance [Bri14], and regular-expression matching [BI16]. Other applications concerned\np5:223: approximating graph diameter [RV13] and maintaining reachability counts or the size of the largest\np5:224: strongly connected component under graph updates [AW14]. Together these results made SETH a\np5:225: common basis for explaining the apparent optimality of algorithms across quite different problems.\np5:226:    Our refutation of ETH and SETH removes one basis for the hardness claims above. Some\np5:227: of the same lower bounds follow from weaker hypotheses that may still hold. For example, the\np5:228: lower bounds for longest common subsequence and regular-expression matching follow from the\np5:229: Orthogonal Vectors Conjecture (OVC) [BK15, BI16]. OVC asserts that for every fixed ε > 0, no\np5:230: O(N 2−ε poly(d))-time algorithm can decide whether two sets of N Boolean vectors of dimension\np5:231: d contain an orthogonal pair, one vector from each set. The reduction from SAT to Orthogonal\np5:232: Vectors shows that SETH implies OVC [Wil05, VW19]. The lower bounds for Fréchet distance,\np5:233: graph diameter approximation, and the treewidth dependence of Independent Set and Dominating\np5:234: Set also follow from the hypothesis that unrestricted CNF-SAT admits no 2(1−δ)n poly(n, m)-time\np5:235: algorithm for any constant δ > 0 [Bri14, RV13, LMS18]. Theorem 1.1 gives a running-time guarantee\np5:236: for each fixed clause width k, while this hypothesis allows the width to grow with n.\np5:237:    To obtain a faster algorithm for a target problem from our SAT algorithm, we need a reduction in\np5:238: the other direction, from that problem to fixed-width SAT, that controls the number of variables.\np5:239: Set Splitting with sets of size at most k has such a reduction [CDL+ 16, Observation 3.6]: use one\np5:240: Boolean variable per element to specify its side of the split. For each set, one clause requires at least\np5:241: one of its variables to be true, and a second requires at least one to be false. The resulting k-CNF\np5:242: has one variable per universe element, so for fixed k Theorem 1.1 gives subexponential time in the\np5:243: universe size, up to a polynomial input-size factor.\np5:244: 1.1.4. Prior work on algebraic algorithms for combinatorial problems. Our algorithm is algebraic,\np5:245: based on polynomial interpolation of carefully chosen auxiliary variables. Below we discuss prior work\np5:246: on using algebraic methods to design algorithm for combinatorial problems. For broader background,\np5:247: see the surveys of Koutis and Williams [KW16] on algebraic fingerprints, Williams [Wil14] on the\np5:248: polynomial method in algorithm design, and Husfeldt [Hus11] on algorithmic inclusion–exclusion.\np5:249:    Williams’s split-and-list algorithm [Wil05] partitions the variables of a 2-CSP into three groups,\np5:250: enumerates partial assignments, and combines them using fast matrix multiplication. Koivisto [Koi06]\np5:251: gives a direct sum–product formulation. The algorithm of Sections 3–6 likewise enumerates as-\np5:252: signments within groups. Fixing the shared measurements ⃗y makes their contributions multiply\np5:253: independently. Scott and Sorkin [SS09, Section 6] extend split-and-list to polynomial-valued weights\np5:254: and arbitrary rings. Our summation routine similarly uses truncated polynomial rings to retain\np5:255: several coefficients in one computation.\np5:256:    Björklund [Bjö16, Sections 2–3] uses inclusion–exclusion and polynomial interpolation to reduce\np5:257: permanent and directed Hamiltonian-cycle counting to a weighted sum over smaller instances. He\np5:258: adds the weights of identical instances in a table, then solves each distinct instance with nonzero\np5:259: total weight once. Björklund, Kaski, and Williams [BKW19] use low-degree curves and generalized\np5:260: \f6                                              OPENAI\np6:261: \np6:262: \np6:263: Kakeya sets to recover polynomial values from evaluations on a small structured set, yielding faster\np6:264: algorithms for fermionants, including the permanent of integer matrices whose entries are bounded\np6:265: in absolute value by a fixed constant. Our polynomial replacements serve a similar purpose: they\np6:266: connect the desired count to structured evaluations from which it can be reconstructed. Here the\np6:267: useful structure is separation into groups after fixing a few shared measurements.\np6:268:    Bhargava, Ghosh, Kumar, and Mohapatra [BGKM23, Sections 3.1–3.2] evaluate multivariate\np6:269: polynomials over finite fields along low-degree curves, using Hasse derivatives and Hermite interpola-\np6:270: tion when values alone do not suffice. For a univariate restriction g, the order-j Hasse derivative at\np6:271: t is the coefficient of z j in g(t + z). We use the same recovery principle: compute several Taylor\np6:272: coefficients at each point admitting fast summation, then recover the original count by Hermite\np6:273: interpolation.\np6:274:    Woodruff and Yekhanin [WY07] use values and first derivatives along a line to recover a target\np6:275: value in private information retrieval. Kopparty, Saraf, and Yekhanin [KSY14] use higher-order\np6:276: Hasse derivatives for local correction of multiplicity codes, extending the line-based recovery of\np6:277: Reed–Muller codes. In our algorithm, the matrix construction supplies low-degree replacement forms\np6:278: that let us compute the Taylor coefficients at the interpolation points by fast summation after fixing\np6:279: a few shared measurements.\np6:280: 1.1.5. Antecedents of the matrix construction. Our counting algorithm evaluates sums of products\np6:281: of polynomials over Boolean assignments. The polynomials share variables, so these sums do not\np6:282: factor into independent smaller sums. We introduce linear substitutions, depending polynomially on\np6:283: an interpolation parameter, that make such a factorization possible after fixing a few integer linear\np6:284: measurements. An auxiliary square rational matrix M0 underlies these substitutions: a bound on\np6:285: rank(I − M0 ) limits the number of measurements, while restrictions on the nonzero entries of M0\np6:286: keep the substitution degrees small. Section 2.7 explains this connection.\np6:287:    Related interpolation methods have been used to obtain low-rank approximations to structured\np6:288: matrices. In [AW17, Section 3], a low-degree polynomial matches (−1)j on an interval of integer\np6:289: inner products j, using the interpolation lemma of [AW15, Lemma 3.1]. This gives low-rank\np6:290: matrices agreeing with Walsh–Hadamard matrices in most entries, a result in the study of matrix\np6:291: rigidity [Val77]. Related interpolation gives rigidity bounds for Kronecker powers in [AGP23,\np6:292: Theorem 6.1]. Dvir and Liu [DL20, Claim 2.20 and Lemmas 3.3–3.4] use a spectral approach: sparse\np6:293: changes to a function invariant under permutations of its coordinates make many Fourier coefficients\np6:294: vanish, reducing the rank of its group-circulant matrix.\np6:295:    Our construction also interpolates (−1)j on a short interval, now to obtain a polynomial in\np6:296: commuting permutation matrices. The same polynomial gives both the rank bound for I − M0\np6:297: and the restrictions on the nonzero entries of M0 . We prove these properties and their quantitative\np6:298: bounds directly in Section 4.\np6:299: 1.1.6. Sparsification and parity tests. We use the counting-preserving version [DHM+ 14, Appendix A]\np6:300: of the sparsification lemma [IPZ01], with quantitative bounds from [CIP06, Section 6] and [Cal09,\np6:301: Lemma 5.14 and equation (5.4)]. This partitions the satisfying assignments among sparse formulas\np6:302: on the same Boolean variables. Appendix A records the disjoint branching argument and the bounds\np6:303: needed when its parameter varies with the input size.\np6:304:    The initial parity tests are the constraints of a Tanner code [Tan81, Section II], applied to the\np6:305: binary vector recording which clauses an assignment violates. We use the edge-based expander-code\np6:306: construction of [SS96, Sections IV and VI]: each graph edge carries one bit, and each vertex requires\np6:307: its incident bits to belong to a fixed binary linear code. The local code’s minimum distance, together\np6:308: with graph expansion, forces every nonzero vector satisfying all these constraints to have large\np6:309: support. This construction and distance argument are standard. Lemma 6.1 supplies the parameter\np6:310: choices needed here, including the bounds on the number and support size of the tests, and constructs\np6:311: the graph and local code by deterministic exhaustive searches. The graph is the bipartite double\np6:312: \f                               THE EXPONENTIAL TIME HYPOTHESIS IS FALSE                                          7\np7:313: \np7:314: \np7:315: cover of a Cayley multigraph. The shifts defining that multigraph nearly balance every nonconstant\np7:316: parity on the base group: this is the small-bias condition [NN93, AR94], stated explicitly in (6.2).\np7:317: Fixing unused edge bits to zero gives a code on exactly m coordinates. Additional parity tests\np7:318: exclude the remaining nonzero violation vectors. We choose these tests by the averaging principle\np7:319: underlying the method of conditional expectations [Rag88], using exact counts to evaluate each\np7:320: candidate. Section 6 gives the constructions and quantitative bounds needed here.\np7:321:    Theorem 1.1 invites a renewed search for algorithms for problems whose known hardness results\np7:322: relied on ETH or SETH. The examples in Section 1.1.3 suggest both immediate consequences and\np7:323: problems for which new algorithmic ideas are needed.\np7:324: \np7:325: 1.2. Notation. For an integer s ⩾ 0, write [s] = {1, . . . , s}, with [0] = ∅. For a condition E,\np7:326: the scalar 1{E} equals one if E holds and zero otherwise. We use natural logarithms, writing\np7:327: log2 when the base is two. For complexity bounds, poly(t1 , . . . , ts ) denotes a bound of the form\np7:328: (1+t1 +· · ·+ts )O(1) , with absolute implicit constants. Subscripts, as in polyk,c (t1 , . . . , ts ), allow those\np7:329: constants to depend on the indicated fixed parameters. Integers are stored in signed binary notation,\np7:330: so an integer z uses O(log(|z| + 2)) bits. Rational numbers are stored as an integer numerator and a","stderr":"","inte