ooses the smallest possible c, and then the largest possible |H|; break any remaining\np44:2538: ties deterministically. Thus a nonempty shared literal set H leaving j = c − |H| other literals must\np44:2539: occur in at least M j clauses before we branch on it.\np44:2540: \f                             THE EXPONENTIAL TIME HYPOTHESIS IS FALSE                                   45\np45:2541: \np45:2542: \np45:2543:    Let H ∪ P1 , . . . , H ∪ Pz be all the width-c clauses containing the chosen H, and let G consist of\np45:2544: the other clauses. Put j = c − |H|. Here Pa is the set of literals left after removing H from the ath\np45:2545: selected clause, so each Pa has width j and z ⩾ θj . The two children are\np45:2546:                                                            z\np45:2547:                                                            ^            ^\np45:2548:                             FA = G ∧ H,        FB = G ∧          Pa ∧         {¬ℓ}.                  (A.2)\np45:2549:                                                            a=1          ℓ∈H\np45:2550: \np45:2551: We restore the antichain condition in each child by removing clauses made redundant by its new\np45:2552: clauses. The sets Pa may overlap. The satisfying assignments of the children are disjoint: FA requires\np45:2553: some literal of H to be true, whereas FB requires every literal of H to be false.\np45:2554: Lemma A.1. The children in (A.2) partition the satisfying assignments of their parent. Every new\np45:2555: clause has width less than c. The A-child inserts one new clause, and the B-child inserts at least θj\np45:2556: new clauses of width j. All newly inserted clauses survive the immediate normalization. Every old\np45:2557: clause removed in either child contains a newly inserted strict subset.\np45:2558: Proof. Every assignment satisfies exactly one of the conditions some literal of H is true and every\np45:2559: literal of H is false. In the first case, all selected cla