{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Myu6DAipMrXrW6X7Xdzt6m","type":"tool_result","content":"p51:2900: has polynomial bit cost for these Taylor data as well. Finally, the enumerations can be performed\np51:2901: sequentially, retaining only the construction, current indices, coefficient arrays, running sums, and\np51:2902: the polynomially many Taylor coefficients needed for interpolation. This gives polynomial space and\np51:2903: only polynomial bit-operation overhead.\np51:2904: \np51:2905:                 Appendix C. Additional Consequences of Refuting ETH\np51:2906:    Theorem 1.1 gives a circuit lower bound and subexponential-time algorithms for inverting Boolean\np51:2907: maps with constant output locality. The fixed-density case gives the quantitative circuit lower bound\np51:2908: in Corollary 1.4. Both circuit arguments use only the decision version of our 3-SAT algorithm. Exact\np51:2909: preimage counting uses its full counting guarantee.\np51:2910: C.1. Circuit lower bounds for ENP . For the qualitative bound below, write SIZE(O(n)) for the\np51:2911: languages having nonuniform circuits with O(n) bounded-fan-in AND, OR, and NOT gates.\np51:2912: Corollary C.1. We have\np51:2913:                                          ENP ̸⊆ SIZE(O(n)).\np51:2914: Proof. Williams proved that if ETH is false, then ENP does not have linear-size circuits [Wil13,\np51:2915: Theorem 6.1]. Apply this implication to the deterministic subexponential-time 3-SAT algorithm\np51:2916: supplied by Theorem 1.1.                                                                      □\np51:2917:    The connection is an instance of deriving circuit lower bounds from faster satisfiability algorithms.\np51:2918: To encode a Boolean circuit with s gates and n inputs, introduce a variable for each gate, enforce\np51:2919: its value from its inputs, and add a unit clause requiring the output bit to equal 1. The resulting\np51:2920: 3-CNF has O(n + s) variables and clauses and is satisfiable exactly when the circuit is. Our theorem\np51:2921: therefore decides satisfiability of linear-size circuits in 2o(n) time. Williams’s argument combines\np51:2922: such a speedup with hypothetical small circuits for witnesses to contradict a nondeterministic time\np51:2923: hierarchy. The corollary gives a language in ENP without a linear-size circuit family; it does not\np51:2924: assert a superpolynomial circuit lower bound for NP.\np51:2925: Proof of Corollary 1.4. We use Williams’s witness-circuit argument [Wil13, Theorem 6.1] with the\np51:2926: local reduction of Jahanjou, Miles, and Viola [JMV13, Theorem 1 and Section 6].\np51:2927:    By Žák’s nondeterministic time hierarchy [Žák83], in the form stated in [Wil13, Theorem 2.1\np51:2928: in the full version], fix a unary language H ∈ NTIME(2n ) \\ NTIME(2n/2 ) on multitape Turing\np51:2929: machines. For all sufficiently large n apply the local reduction to its fixed machine on 1n with\np51:2930: locality parameter r = 2. A time allowance T = 2n nO(1) includes the polylogarithmic overhead of\np51:2931: simulating the machine in the reduction and gives q = n + O(log n) clause-index bits. In polynomial\np51:2932: time the reduction constructs a decoder giving three variable labels and their signs on each index.\np51:2933: Let φn consist of the clauses decoded on all 2q indices. It is satisfiable exactly when 1n ∈ H.\np51:2934:    Each of the decoder’s O(n) output bits has a constant-depth decision tree, so it depends on at\np51:2935: most a fixed number w of index bits. Evaluating the decoder on all strings of Hamming weight at\np51:2936: most w recovers each support. Indeed, two strings witnessing dependence on one coordinate can be\np51:2937: \f52                                                    OPENAI\np52:2938: \np52:2939: \np52:2940: set to zero off the support while retaining their differing outputs. The same evaluations give the\np52:2941: truth table on each support. This constructs a decoder of O(n) fan-in-two gates in polynomial time.\np52:2942:     Pad each variable label to dn bits for a fixed positive integer d and declare all 2dn variables. If\np52:2943: |i| = dn for such an n and φn is satisfiable, let W (i) be bit i of its lexicographically first satisfying\np52:2944: assignment, ordering variables by their labels. Set W (i) = 0 in all other cases. The length of i\np52:2945: determines n. To decide W , write out the 2q clauses, ask an NP oracle whether the formula is\np52:2946: satisfiable, and, if so, recover the first assignment by asking whether its current prefix followed by\np52:2947: zero has a satisfying\np52:2948:                 p       extension. At most 2dn prefix queries of length 2O(n) suffice, so W ∈ ENP .\np52:2949:     Put a(t) = log2 t. Testing whether the count in the constant-density part of Theorem 1.1 is\np52:2950: positive decides 3-SAT with V variables and at most 5V clauses in time V b 2AV /a(V ) for fixed A, b > 0.\np52:2951: We absorb the change of logarithm base in A. The solver uses polynomial space, so a multitape\np52:2952: simulation can scan its workspace for each bit access at a cost V O(1) , absorbed in b.\np52:2953:     Fix a positive rational η < 1/(12A) and put sn = ⌊ηn⌊a(n)⌋⌋ for n ⩾ 2. The threshold is\np52:2954: computable using the binary length of n and the integer square root of ⌊log2 n⌋. Suppose that\np52:2955: CW (dn) ⩽ sn for all sufficiently large n. To decide H, reject nonunary inputs. On 1n , guess a circuit\np52:2956: on dn index bits with at most sn gates. Feed the decoder’s three variable labels to three copies of\np52:2957: this circuit and test whether the indexed clause is violated. Accept if the test circuit is unsatisfiable.\np52:2958: When 1n ∈ H, a circuit computing W at length dn gives an accepting guess. When 1n ∈            / H, every\np52:2959: guess violates a clause.\np52:2960:     The test circuit has q inputs and at most 3sn + O(n) gates. The encoding above uses one variable\np52:2961: per input and gate, at most four clauses of width at most three per gate to enforce its truth table,\np52:2962: and one unit clause to pin the output. Padding with unused variables gives Un = 3sn + Bn variables\np52:2963: and at most 5Un clauses for a fixed integer B. Since Un = (3η + o(1))na(n) and a(Un ) ∼ a(n),\np52:2964: the test takes 2(3Aη+o(1))n time. Guessing and building the circuits take polynomial time, so H is\np52:2965: decidable nondeterministically in time 2n/2 for all sufficiently large n. The finitely many exceptional\np52:2966: lengths can bephardwired, contradicting the choice of H. Thus CW (dn) >√        sn for infinitely many n.\np52:2967: Since sn ∼ ηn log2 n and d is fixed, taking L = W and any 0 < c < η/(d log 2) proves (1.2). □\np52:2968: C.2. Inverting Boolean maps with constant output locality. Fix a constant d. Consider a\np52:2969: Boolean map G : {0, 1}n → {0, 1}m whose ith output bit has the form\np52:2970:                               G(⃗x)i = gi (⃗x|Si ),      Si ⊆ [n],   |Si | ⩽ d.\np52:2971: The input description of G lists the sets Si and the truth tables of the Boolean functions gi . Thus\np52:2972: each output inspects only constantly many input bits, although the sets Si may overlap arbitrarily.\np52:2973: Given a target ⃗b ∈ {0, 1}m , inversion asks for any ⃗x with G(⃗x) = ⃗b.\np52:2974: Corollary C.2. For every fixed d and n ⩾ 20, the number |G−1 (⃗b)| can be computed exactly, and a\np52:2975: preimage can be found whenever one exists, in deterministic time\np52:2976:                                           (n + m)Od (1) 2Od (ρ(n))\np52:2977: and space (n + m)Od (1) .\np52:2978:                                             α ∈ {0, 1}Si with gi (⃗\np52:2979: Proof. For each i and each local assignment ⃗                     α) ̸= bi , include the clause\np52:2980:                                _\np52:2981:                                    ℓj,αj ,  ℓj,0 = Xj , ℓj,1 = ¬Xj .\np52:2982:     