very vertex and Boolean choice of its ports, that its entry can be computed\np53:3023:                                           9/10\np53:3024: from the description in time (s + L)b 2As      and space (s + L)b . Then the contraction can be computed\np53:3025: deterministically in                                       √\np53:3026:                                          (s + L)O(1) 2O(s/ log s)\np53:3027: bit operations and (s + L)O(1) bits of space. Constants depend only on the fixed bounds in the\np53:3028: hypotheses. In particular, ℓ = 1 is permitted.\np53:3029:    The algorithm in Section D.4 replaces a single global Taylor expansion by recursion on the wires.\np53:3030: At each call it deforms the wire identifications so that, at a chosen interpolation node, only ports in\np53:3031: the same group interact. Near that node, interactions between groups expand in variables called\np53:3032: modes. A mode of weight j has coefficient divisible by 2j , so only monomials of total weight below\np53:3033: the working precision can contribute. Tapering the Hermite multiplicities with the two-adic valuation\np53:3034: of the node bounds that precision.\np53:3035:                               √        With M wires and u retained valuation levels, the number\np53:3036: of mode monomials is 2O(M/ u) , and each monomial factors into contractions with at most M/8\np53:3037:                                            −d                       d\np53:3038:                       √ d, M = O(s8 ) and u = Θ((log s)/2 ). The exponents therefore sum\np53:3039: wires. At recursion depth\np53:3040: geometrically to O(s/ log s).\np53:3041: \f54                                                 OPENAI\np54:3042: \np54:3043: \np54:3044: D.2. Fixed clause density.\np54:3045: Proof of the fixed-density bound in Theorem 1.1. Give each clause a vertex carrying its Boolean\np54:3046: satisfaction table, with one port for each distinct variable in the clause. A tautology may be dropped,\np54:304