                                                1\np1:18:   2. Technique overview                                                                                       7\np1:19:   3. A fast counting algorithm via shared linear summaries                                                   21\np1:20:   4. Constructing low-degree replacement forms                                                               26\np1:21:   5. Counting common zeros of sparse polynomial equations                                                    35\np1:22:   6. Reducing #k-SAT to counting common zeros                                                                39\np1:23:   Appendix A. Partitioning the satisfying assignments                                                        44\np1:24:   Appendix B. Exact arithmetic and space                                                                     47\np1:25:   Appendix C. Additional Consequences of Refuting ETH                                                        51\np1:26:   Appendix D. A sharper bound for exact counting                                                             53\np1:27:   References                                                                                                 64\np1:28: \np1:29: \np1:30: \np1:31:                                                1. Introduction\np1:32:    For every fixed k ⩾ 3, k-SAT is the canonical NP-complete problem [Coo71], and its exponential-\np1:33: time complexity underlies the Exponential Time Hypothesis (ETH) and the Strong Exponential\np1:34: Time Hypothesis (SETH) [IP01, IPZ01]. In this paper, we refute ETH by giving, for every fixed\np1:35: k ⩾ 2, a deterministic algorithm that counts the satisfying assignments of a k-CNF in subexponential\np1:36: time and polynomial space.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_013P9gHtkjckXh1RNpmT5KSK","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reas