n(`exact_count_eth`) does convert `(L+1)^d` to `(n + F.card + 1)^{2d}` for `n ≥ 20`, so the\n`(n+m)` polynomial form is in Lean too; the sharper `log log n` (unsquared) exponent is not.\n\n## What this audit does not establish\n\n- **I did not build, export or replay the proof.** Zero `sorry`/`axiom` by grep is\n  necessary, not sufficient: a file could fail to elaborate, and only `lake build` plus\n  `#print axioms` (or comparator) shows the closure type-checks with the three permitted\n  axioms. The repository offers no recorded evidence of either.\n- I did not read the ~3,000 files of algorithmic content (sparsification, ring\n  arithmetic, DFS) — only the spine from `Final.lean` down to the compiler lemmas. The\n  argument above is that their correctness is kernel-enforced, not that I verified it.\n- Mathlib `d13f23b7` and Lean `v4.34.1` are trusted as upstream; I did not audit either.\n- Whether the comparator statement means what the paper claims (encoding, `runtimeScale`,","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"43cbaf52-3109-48ae-9853-92f13356d98c","uuid":"9304a9c8-0a7a-44e8-87f3-8fdb642e5b66","timestamp":"2026-11-05T02:51:26.071Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"instance variable from a definition's type (`VarCheck.lean`: both `Literal.eval` and\n`Clause.eval` have no `DecidableEq` argument in either variant). `enable_nanoda: false` is\nthe setting on 416 of the 418 challenge JSONs, so no second kernel was run for this one, as\nfor nearly all; the `patches/` directory touches other libraries only, none Mathlib. I could\nnot run the comparator (forbidden); that it passed is the repository's claim, and what this\naudit establishes is that *if* it passed, the statement checked is the one analysed here.\n\n## 8. The paper's companion claims (not audited)\n\nSupported by the Lean statement (they need only a deterministic `poly(n+m)·2^{O_k(ρ(n))}`\n#k-SAT counter): refutation of ETH, SETH, Gap-ETH, NSETH, #ETH and their bounded-error\nrandomised 