 (item 02): `python3 -I\n  paper3/scripts/runtime_scale.py` prints the `ρ(n)/n` table (Table 2.1),\n  the first integer above 1 (`n = 50`: `ρ(49)/49 = 0.9994`,\n  `ρ(50)/50 = 1.0027`), the peak (`16/e² = 2.1654` at `ln(n+16) = e⁴`,\n  `n ≈ 10^23.71`) and the return below 1 (`ln(n+16) = 5503.7`,\n  `n ≈ e^5504 ≈ 10^2390`), and writes `latex/tables/runtime_scale.tex` and\n  `latex/figures/runtime_scale.{pdf,png}` (Figure 2.2). `python3 -I\n  paper3/scripts/rho_over_n.py` draws the central figure (Figure 2.1, two\n  panels, with the manuscript's two unverified exponents beneath) and\n  writes `latex/tables/rho_over_n.csv`. The encoding lengths quoted in\n  §2.1 (`L = 1,006,223` at `n, m, k = 125, 500, 4`; `47.9 M` at\n  `1000, 4000, 3`) come from `python3 -I\n  reports/scratch/eth_audit/check_encoding.py`. Every comparator line\n  number is at clone commit `4eaee875`; every manuscript quote gives the\n  page of `paper.pdf` and the line of `reports/scratch/eth_audit/paper.txt`.\n\n## The replay\n== sec5 84-90\nkept out of the repository by size; its SHA-256\n(\\texttt{d313f594…}) and its last 200 lines are committed.\n\nSo the sentence every later section relies on reads: \\emph{replayed}---the\nsolution's closure builds from source at the pinned toolchain with\nupstream Mathlib, and the kernel reports the three standard axioms and\nnothing else for the theorem the comparator names. This moves the first\n== rho_over_n sharper\n6:exponent relative to brute force at A = 1.  Also drawn: the manuscript's two sharper,\n23:def rho_verified(n):            # comparator: runtimeScale n\n24:    return n * math.log(math.log(n + 16)) ** 2 / math.sqrt(math.log(n + 16))\n25:def rho_paper(n):               # Theorem 1.1, general k-CNF (not verified)\n26:    return n * math.log(math.log(n)) / math.sqrt(math.log(n))\n27:def rho_fixed_density(n):       # Theorem 1.1, m <= D n (not verified)\n28:    return n / math.sqrt(math.log(n))\n33:def safe(f, n):\n39:def ratios(l10):\n40:    ln = l10 * math.log(10.0)\n45:    return (math.log(ln) ** 2 / math.sqrt(ln), math.log(ln) / math.sqrt(ln), 1.0 / math.sqrt(ln))\n51:peak_l10, peak_v = math.e ** 4 / math.log(10.0), 16.0 / math.e ** 2\n53:cross_ln = 5504.0; cross_l10 = cross_ln / math.log(10.0)\n== docs/378.md numbered\n     1\t# The Exponential Time Hypothesis is False\n     2\t\n     3\tThe following describes the scope of the Lean formalization related to the following accompanying paper(s):\n     4\t\n     5\t- [The Exponential Time Hypothesis is False](../../preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/paper.pdf)\n     6\t\n     7\t## Scope\n     8\t\n     9\tFor fixed $k\\ge2$, Lean proves that a deterministic machine exactly counts satisfying assignments of a canonically encoded $k$-CNF on $n$ variables in time $(L+1)^{O_k(1)}2^{O_k(n(\\log\\log n)^2/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$, where $L$ is the encoding's bit length. At every step, the machine has visited at most $(L+1)^{O_k(1)}$ tape cells in total.\n    10\t\n    11\tFor $m$ clauses, the October 25 manuscript gives the sharper time bound $(n+m)^{O_k(1)}2^{O_k(n\\log\\log n/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$. When $m\\le Dn$ for fixed $D>0$, this improves to $(n+m)^{O_{k,D}(1)}2^{O_{k,D}(n/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$.\n    12\t\n    13\t## Comparator links\n    14\t\n    15\t| Result | Comparator statement |\n    16\t| --- | --- |\n    17\t| Exact counting and SAT on canonical inputs in subexponential time and polynomial space | [SubexponentialSAT.lean](../ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean) |\n== 378.md cited where\npaper3/latex/sec2_statement.tex:11:The whole of what is machine-checked about family 378 is one theorem,","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"43cbaf52-3109-48ae-9853-92f13356d98c","uuid":"c6753f3d-cfe6-49cf-98d5-59b6ea191a58","timestamp":"2026-11-05T02:53:33.320Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"== tail pages\np67:3795: [VW19]    Virginia Vassilevska Williams. On Some Fine-Grained Question\np67:3796:           of the 