{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011Cgj2p2A8qvxMo9DMjMgUg","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017Qjz7a44pxssUSpmCvGnb3","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/al/dev/MOSP/paper3/latex/sec3_beyond.tex","content":"% What the manuscript claims beyond the verified statement -- drafted by loop0015\n% item 03 (2026-11-05). Every manuscript quotation gives the page of paper.pdf and\n% the line of reports/scratch/eth_audit/paper.txt (page = form feeds before the\n% line + 1); every Lean location is in the clone at commit 4eaee875, read with\n% cat/sed -n only. Every page, line, fragment and number of this section is\n% checked and the two tables are written by\n%   python3 -I paper3/scripts/beyond_claims.py\n% which exits non-zero if anything has moved.\n\\section{What the manuscript claims beyond the verified statement}\n\\label{sec:beyond}\n\nThe manuscript \\citep{OAI:The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026}\nis 67 pages; the comparator statement is 220 lines. Section~\\ref{sec:statement}\nread the statement. This section reads the manuscript's claims one by one\nand says, for each, whether it is what the Lean theorem asserts, whether it\nfollows from the Lean theorem by an argument that exists only on paper, or\nwhether it rests on a bound that the Lean library does not contain. The\ncollection's own scope note draws the line in two sentences, which we quote\nin full (\\clonefile{lean/docs/378.md}, lines 9 and 11):\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n``For fixed $k \\ge 2$, Lean proves that a deterministic machine exactly\ncounts satisfying assignments of a canonically encoded $k$-CNF on $n$\nvariables in time $(L+1)^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{O_k(n(\\log\\log n)^2/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$,\nwhere $L$ is the encoding's bit length. At every step, the machine has\nvisited at most $(L+1)^{O_k(1)}$ tape cells in total.''\n\n``For $m$ clauses, the October 25 manuscript gives the sharper time bound\n$(n+m)^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{O_k(n\\log\\log n/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$. When $m \\le Dn$ for\nfixed $D > 0$, this improves to\n$(n+m)^{O_{k,D}(1)}\\,2^{O_{k,D}(n/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$.''\n\\end{quote}\nEverything below is an elaboration of that note against the manuscript's\ntext and the library's files. Table~\\ref{tab:beyond-claims} is the summary.\n\n\\subsection{Three statuses, and what ``paper-only'' means}\n\\label{sec:beyond-statuses}\n\nWe tag every claim with one of three words.\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{Verified.} The claim is the comparator statement\n  \\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat}, or one of its conjuncts, or a theorem of\n  the solution tree that the replay of Section~\\ref{sec:replay} compiled\n  and whose axioms it printed. Nothing else is called verified.\n\\item \\textbf{Supported.} The claim follows from the verified statement by\n  an argument that is on paper only: a reduction of a few lines, or a\n  citation. The argument is not in the Lean library, and we have not\n  checked it; what we have checked is that the only property of the\n  counter it uses is one the verified statement provides, and that the\n  exponent it inherits is therefore $\\rhon$, not a sharper one.\n\\item \\textbf{Paper-only.} The claim rests on a bound that the Lean\n  library at commit \\texttt{4eaee875} does not contain: Theorem~1.1's two\n  sharper exponents, and whatever is derived from them.\n\\end{itemize}\n\nThe brief for this section had two tags, verified and paper-only; the\nmiddle one is ours, because the manuscript's corollaries are of two\ndifferent kinds and a single word would have put Corollary~1.3 (a\ncounting-preserving encoding into the verified counter) beside\nCorollary~1.4 (a bound whose proof calls the fixed-density exponent by\nname). In the brief's partition, ``supported'' is a sub-case of\n``paper-only'': not in the library. In no case does ``paper-only'' mean\n``wrong''. We did not read the manuscript's proofs for correctness; the\ntag records where a claim is established, not whether.\n\n\\subsection{The sharper exponents of Theorem~1.1}\n\\label{sec:beyond-exponents}\n\nTheorem~1.1 (p.~2, lines 56--62) states two running-time bounds for the\nsame deterministic algorithm: $(n+m)^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{O_k(n\\log\\log n/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$\nfor every $k$-CNF on $n \\ge 20$ declared variables, and\n$(n+m)^{O_{k,D}(1)}\\,2^{O_{k,D}(n/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$ when $m \\le Dn$ for a\nfixed $D > 0$; both with $(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$ bits of space (line 60). The\nsentence that follows the theorem is the one the title of this paper is\nabout (p.~2, lines 63--67):\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n``The rational interpolation argument in Section 6.4 gives the weaker\nexponent $O_k(\\rho(n))$, where\n$\\rho(n) = n(\\log\\log n)^2/\\sqrt{\\log n} = n/(\\log n)^{1/2+o(1)}$.\nAppendix D proves both bounds in Theorem 1.1 by tensor contraction.''\n\\end{quote}\nProposition~6.4 (p.~43, lines 2474--2476) is the weaker bound as a\nstatement of its own: for each fixed $k \\ge 2$, the interpolation\nalgorithm counts in $(n+m)^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{O_k(\\rho(n))}$ bit operations and\n$(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$ bits of space, for $n \\ge 20$.\n\n\\paragraph{Which of the three is in the Lean.}\nThe comparator's \\runtimeScale{} (lines 132--134 of the comparator file)\nis $n(\\ln\\ln(n+16))^2/\\sqrt{\\ln(n+16)}$, the squared form. The solution's\nown base-2 exponent \\texttt{rho}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Asymptotics.lean}, lines 10--11) is\n$n(\\log_2\\log_2 n)^2/\\sqrt{\\log_2 n}$, Proposition~6.4's $\\rho$ with the\nbase made explicit, and \\texttt{runtimeScale\\_le\\_four\\_rho} (lines\n190--191) bounds the comparator's exponent by four times it for\n$n \\ge 20$. The solution's sparsification parameters are Proposition~6.4's\nto the letter: the proof selects $\\lambda_n = \\lceil\\log_2 n\\rceil$,\n$\\nu_n = \\lceil\\log_2\\lambda_n\\rceil$ and\n$s = \\lfloor\\sqrt{\\lambda_n}/\\nu_n^2\\rfloor$, ``the largest nonnegative\ninteger satisfying $s^2\\nu_n^4 \\le \\lambda_n$'' (p.~43, lines 2480--2486),\nand \\path{Asymptotics.lean} lines 14--20 define \\texttt{sparsifyLambda},\n\\texttt{sparsifyNu} and \\texttt{sparsifySchedule} as exactly those three\nquantities in natural-number arithmetic. So the verified exponent is the\nweaker one, and the verified proof is organised around Section~6.4's\nparameters; what Appendix~D proves is not in the library in any form we\ncould find (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond-lean}). The general exponent\n$n\\log\\log n/\\sqrt{\\log n}$ and the fixed-density exponent\n$n/\\sqrt{\\log n}$ are \\textbf{paper-only}: proved at p.~57 (lines\n3222--3246) and p.~54 (lines 3045--3064) respectively, by the contraction\ntheorem of Appendix~D.\n\n\\paragraph{Both bounds from one algorithm.}\nThe manuscript notes that a single algorithm attains both paper-only\nbounds, switching on $m \\le n\\nu$ (p.~57, lines 3247--3250). That\nalgorithm is not the verified one either.\n\n\\paragraph{The three exponents, in numbers.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:beyond-exponents} puts the three exponents side by side,\neach divided by $n$ and each at constant $1$. The verified ratio is the\none of Table~\\ref{tab:runtime-scale}. The general paper-only ratio,\n$\\ln\\ln n/\\sqrt{\\ln n}$, never reaches $1$: it peaks at $2/e = 0.736$\nwhere $\\ln n = e^2$, that is $n = e^{e^2} \\approx 1{,}618$, and falls on\nboth sides. The fixed-density ratio $1/\\sqrt{\\ln n}$ decreases from\n$0.578$ at $n = 20$. So, at constant $1$, both paper-only guarantees sit\nbelow $2^n$ at every size, and the verified one sits above $2^n$ from\n$n = 50$ to $n \\approx 10^{2390}$. That is the whole content of the\ntitle's qualifier ``verified'': the manuscript's exponents would make a\ndifferent sentence, and they are not what the kernel checked. Two\ncautions apply to the table equally. The constants in front of every\nexponent are $O_k(1)$ or $O_{k,D}(1)$ and are not computed anywhere, in\nthe manuscript or in the Lean; and the manuscript's exponents are\nundefined below $n = e$ and are stated for $n \\ge 20$, while the\nverified one is stated for all $n$ (the $+16$). Figure~\\ref{fig:central}\ndraws the same three curves.\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\caption{The three exponents of the manuscript divided by $n$, at constant\n$1$: the verified $\\rhon/n = (\\ln\\ln(n+16))^2/\\sqrt{\\ln(n+16)}$\n(comparator lines 132--134); Theorem~1.1's general exponent\n$\\ln\\ln n/\\sqrt{\\ln n}$ (p.~2, line 59); its fixed-density exponent\n$1/\\sqrt{\\ln n}$ (p.~2, line 62). Natural logarithms throughout (p.~1,\nlines 48--49). The row $e^{e^2}$ is the peak of the general ratio\n($2/e$). Regenerate: \\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/beyond\\_claims.py}.}\n\\label{tab:beyond-exponents}\n\\small\n\\input{tables/beyond_exponents}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{The abstract's form.}\nThe abstract states the running time as\n$(n+m)^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{n/(\\log n)^{1/2+o(1)}}$ (p.~1, lines 6--9). Since\n$(\\ln\\ln n)^2 = (\\ln n)^{o(1)}$, the verified exponent is of that form\ntoo, and the manuscript says so at lines 64--66. We tag the abstract's\nform \\textbf{verified}, with the remark that its $o(1)$ absorbs exactly\nthe factor the three rows of Table~\\ref{tab:beyond-exponents} differ by:\nat $n = 10^6$ it is $(\\ln\\ln n)^2 = 6.9$ for the verified exponent,\n$\\ln\\ln n = 2.6$ for the general one and $1$ for the fixed-density one.\n\n\\subsection{The corollaries}\n\\label{sec:beyond-corollaries}\n\n\\paragraph{Finding and sampling a satisfying assignment (p.~2, lines 68--71).}\n``When $\\#F > 0$, the standard self-reduction finds a satisfying\nassignment by fixing one variable at a time and checking that the\nremaining count is positive. Choosing each bit according to the two\nconditional counts instead gives an exactly uniform random satisfying\nassignment,'' with polynomial expected overhead and a citation.\n\\textbf{Supported}: the self-reduction calls the verified counter at\nmost $n$ times on formulas of the same width, and the verified statement\nprovides the exact count it needs. The reduction is two sentences of\nprose and is not in the library.\n\n\\paragraph{Corollary 1.2, Max-$k$-SAT within $\\varepsilon m$ (p.~2, lines 77--94).}\nA randomised algorithm finds an assignment satisfying at least\n$\\mathrm{OPT}(F) - \\varepsilon m$ clauses with probability\n$1 - 2^{-\\Omega(n)}$ in $(n+m)^{O_{k,\\varepsilon}(1)}\\,2^{O_{k,\\varepsilon}(\\rho(n))}$\ntime (line 80). The corollary is stated with $\\rho(n)$ itself, the\nverified exponent. Its proof samples $O(n/\\varepsilon^2)$ clauses,\nbuilds a threshold circuit of size $O_{k,\\varepsilon}(n)$, reduces it to\na 3-CNF with $O_{k,\\varepsilon}(n)$ variables by a cited lemma, and calls\nTheorem~1.1 with binary search and self-reduction (lines 82--94).\n\\textbf{Supported}: the exponent matches the verified one because\n$\\rho(cn) = O_c(\\rho(n))$, and every call is to a fixed-width counter\non $O(n)$ variables; the sampling, the circuit and the reduction are on\npaper. Its randomness is in the reduction, not in the counter.\n\n\\paragraph{Corollary 1.3, independent sets, colourings, perfect matchings (p.~2--3, lines 100--120).}\nThe number of independent sets and, for fixed $q$, of proper\n$q$-colourings of a graph on $v$ vertices and $e$ edges in $2^{o(v)}$\ntime, and the number of perfect matchings in $2^{o(v+e)}$ time, all in\npolynomial space. The encodings are explicit: one variable per vertex\nand a 2-clause per edge (lines 107--112); $q$ variables per vertex with\nexactly-one and no-equal-colour clauses, a $q$-CNF on $qv$ variables\n(lines 112--115); one variable per edge and a linear-size circuit for\n``exactly one selected edge meets each vertex'', reduced to 3-CNF with\n$O(v+e)$ variables, whose gate variables are determined by the edge\nbits so that the count is preserved (lines 116--119).\n\\textbf{Supported}: the conclusion is $2^{o(\\cdot)}$, which needs only\n$\\rhon = o(n)$ \\leanref{rho-little-o}, and each encoding is\ncounting-preserving into a fixed-width CNF on $O(v)$ or $O(v+e)$\nvariables. None of the three encodings is in the library.\n\n\\paragraph{Corollary 1.4, a circuit lower bound for $\\mathrm{E}^{\\mathrm{NP}}$ (p.~3, lines 121--127).}\n``The fixed-density case of Theorem 1.1 gives a circuit lower bound for\n$\\mathrm{E}^{\\mathrm{NP}}$'' (line 121): a language $L \\in \\mathrm{E}^{\\mathrm{NP}}$\nand a constant $c > 0$ with $C_L(n) > c\\,n\\sqrt{\\log n}$ for infinitely\nmany $n$, where $C_L(n)$ counts non-input fan-in-two gates (lines\n123--127). The proof is Appendix~C.1 (p.~51--52, lines 2925--2967),\nWilliams's witness-circuit argument with a local reduction, and it\ninvokes the paper-only exponent in so many words: ``Put\n$a(t) = \\sqrt{\\log_2 t}$. Testing whether the count in the\nconstant-density part of Theorem 1.1 is positive decides 3-SAT with $V$\nvariables and at most $5V$ clauses in time $V^b\\,2^{AV/a(V)}$ for fixed\n$A, b > 0$'' (p.~52, lines 2949--2950); the threshold\n$s_n = \\lfloor\\eta n\\lfloor a(n)\\rfloor\\rfloor$ is chosen against that\n$a$ (line 2953), and the constant $c$ comes out as\n$\\sqrt{\\eta/(d\\log 2)}$ (line 2967). \\textbf{Paper-only}: the\nquantitative bound $c\\,n\\sqrt{\\log n}$ is tied to $2^{AV/\\sqrt{\\log V}}$,\nwhich the library does not contain. What the same argument would give\nfrom the verified exponent is a question about the manuscript's proof,\nwhich we do not examine (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond-not}).\n\n\\paragraph{Corollary C.1, $\\mathrm{E}^{\\mathrm{NP}} \\not\\subseteq \\mathrm{SIZE}(O(n))$ (p.~51, lines 2912--2916).}\n``Williams proved that if ETH is false, then $\\mathrm{E}^{\\mathrm{NP}}$\ndoes not have linear-size circuits [Wil13, Theorem 6.1]. Apply this\nimplication to the deterministic subexponential-time 3-SAT algorithm\nsupplied by Theorem 1.1.'' \\textbf{Supported}: the only input is a\ndeterministic $2^{o(n)}$ 3-SAT decision, which the verified statement\nprovides on its answer tape for $k = 3$; the implication is a citation.\nThe manuscript's own scope sentence applies: the corollary ``does not\nassert a superpolynomial circuit lower bound for NP'' (lines 2923--2924).\n\n\\paragraph{Corollary C.2, inverting constant-locality maps (p.~52, lines 2974--2977).}\nFor a Boolean map whose every output bit reads at most $d$ input bits,\nthe preimage count $|G^{-1}(\\vec b)|$ and a preimage in deterministic\ntime $(n+m)^{O_d(1)}\\,2^{O_d(\\rho(n))}$ and polynomial space, for\n$n \\ge 20$. Stated with $\\rho(n)$. The proof writes one clause per\nforbidden local assignment, a CNF of width at most $d$ on the original\n$n$ variables with at most $2^d m$ clauses whose satisfying assignments\nare exactly the preimages, and applies Theorem~1.1 with\n$k = \\max\\{2, d\\}$ (lines 2979--2988). \\textbf{Supported}: the\nencoding is on paper; the counter and the exponent are the verified\nones. The remark that follows, on Goldreich's candidate one-way\nfunctions (lines 2994--2999), inherits the same status and the\nmanuscript's own limit, ``This does not rule out one-wayness against\npolynomial-time algorithms'' (line 2999).\n\n\\subsection{Appendix D: the contraction route}\n\\label{sec:beyond-appendix-d}\n\nAppendix~D, ``A sharper bound for exact counting'' (p.~53--64, from line\n3001), proves Theorem~1.1 ``by contracting Boolean tensor networks. The\ntwo running times reflect the number of wires in the networks'' (lines\n3002--3003). Theorem~D.1 (p.~53, lines 3017--3028) is the contraction\ntheorem: a bipartite tensor network with at most $Cs$ edges, maximum\ndegree at most $\\lceil s^{9/10}\\rceil$ and entries in\n$(\\mathbb{Z}/2^{P_0}\\mathbb{Z})[V]/(V^\\ell)$ is contracted in\n$(s+L)^{O(1)}\\,2^{O(s/\\sqrt{\\log s})}$ bit operations and polynomial\nspace. The fixed-density proof (p.~54, lines 3045--3064) encodes a\nformula as a network with $O_k(n+m)$ edges and applies Theorem~D.1 with\n$s = n + m$; the arbitrary-density proof (p.~57, lines 3222--3246)\ncombines the sparsification of Section~6 with Lemma~D.2 (p.~54, line\n3069) and reaches the exponent $O_k(n\\log\\log n/\\sqrt{\\log n})$ at line\n3243. Section~D.4 changes the logarithm convention for its own proof,\n``log denotes a base-two logarithm and ln denotes a natural logarithm''\n(p.~57, lines 3251--3252). All of this is \\textbf{paper-only}: no\ndeclaration about tensor networks, contraction, Hermite recovery or\npackets exists in the family's 3,977 files, by the identifier search of\nSection~\\ref{sec:beyond-lean}. We make no statement about Appendix~D's\nargument; we did not read it for correctness.\n\n\\subsection{The hypotheses of \\S1.1.1 and the fine-grained remarks of \\S1.1.3}\n\\label{sec:beyond-hypotheses}\n\n\\paragraph{\\S1.1.1 (p.~4, lines 187--191).}\n``Theorem 1.1 refutes ETH, SETH, Gap-ETH, and NSETH. Indeed, testing\nwhether the exact count is positive or zero decides both satisfiability\nand unsatisfiability in deterministic $2^{o(n)}(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$ time for\nevery fixed $k$; deciding satisfiability also solves the Gap-ETH promise\nproblem. The deterministic algorithm also refutes the bounded-error\nrandomized versions of ETH [\\ldots] and SETH [\\ldots], and of Gap-ETH.''\nAnd \\S1.1.2 (p.~5, lines 211--212): ``Our algorithm (Theorem 1.1) solves\n\\#3-SAT deterministically in $2^{o(n)}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$ time and\ntherefore refutes \\#ETH.'' \\textbf{Supported}, and by the shortest\nargument in the manuscript: the verified statement is a deterministic\nmachine whose answer tape carries $[0 < \\mathtt{count}\\ F]$ and whose\ncount tape carries the count, within $2^{o(n)}$ times a polynomial, for\neach fixed $k \\ge 2$; each hypothesis is the negation of that. The\nhypotheses themselves are not defined in the library (the strings\n``ETH'' and ``SETH'' occur in the collection's Lean only in two files of\na different family, \\clonefile{lean/OAI/Combinatorics/ParityLifts/},\nwhich take an ETH-like proposition as a hypothesis and are not in the\nclosure). Section~\\ref{sec:consequences} states each hypothesis with its\nquantifiers and says exactly how the verified statement negates it; the\ntwo sentences the manuscript adds at lines 191--194, on unconditional\nlower bounds for restricted proof systems and on Williams's 2016\nMA-SETH protocol, are context, not claims about the result.\n\n\\paragraph{\\S1.1.3 (p.~5, lines 213--243).}\nAfter a list of lower bounds proved from ETH or SETH (lines 213--225),\nthe manuscript writes: ``Our refutation of ETH and SETH removes one basis\nfor the hardness claims above. Some of the same lower bounds follow from\nweaker hypotheses that may still hold'' (lines 226--227), names the\nOrthogonal Vectors Conjecture (lines 229--232) and the hypothesis that\nunrestricted CNF-SAT admits no $2^{(1-\\delta)n}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$\nalgorithm, adding: ``Theorem 1.1 gives a running-time guarantee for each\nfixed clause width $k$, while this hypothesis allows the width to grow\nwith $n$'' (lines 235--236). These are \\textbf{paper-only} remarks in\nthe sense that nothing about any other hypothesis or any reduction is in\nthe library; they are also the manuscript's own statement of what the\nresult does not do, and Section~\\ref{sec:consequences} rests on them.\nThe one positive transfer, Set Splitting with sets of size at most $k$\nby one variable per element and two clauses per set (lines 237--243), is\n\\textbf{supported} in the same sense as Corollary~1.3: an encoding on\npaper into the verified counter, inheriting $\\rhon$. The caveat at\np.~2, lines 95--99, that $2^{o(n)}$ bounds ``do not transfer under\ngeneral polynomial-time reductions,'' is not a claim but a limit the\nmanuscript places on its own result; it is quoted again in\nSection~\\ref{sec:consequences}.\n\n\\subsection{What the library carries beyond the comparator statement}\n\\label{sec:beyond-lean}\n\n``Paper-only'' is defined against the whole library at commit\n\\texttt{4eaee875}, not only against the comparator file, so we say what\nelse the library proves about this family. \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}\n(201 lines) states five theorems, each followed by a \\texttt{\\#print\naxioms} directive (lines 197--201), and the replay's build log carries\nthe kernel's answer for all five: the three standard axioms\n(\\repofile{paper3/replay/logs/run2/axioms\\_in\\_build.txt}).\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\texttt{compiled\\_exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat} (line 10) and\n  \\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat} (line 74): the comparator statement,\n  first over the explicit 80-register, 1,220-tape program and then over\n  the machine it compiles to (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}).\n\\item \\texttt{exact\\_count\\_eth} (lines 104--147): the same conclusion\n  with the polynomial factor written as $(n + |F| + 1)^{2d}$ instead of\n  $(L+1)^d$, the exponent as $4A\\cdot\\mathtt{rho}\\ n$ in base 2, and the\n  proviso $20 \\le n$ (line 109). This is Proposition~6.4's\n  $(n+m)^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{O_k(\\rho(n))}$ form in the library, so the\n  manuscript's polynomial factor is verified as well as its exponent; it\n  is \\textbf{verified}, and it is still the weaker exponent.\n\\item \\texttt{sat\\_polynomial\\_space} (lines 149--183), for $k \\ge 3$:\n  the answer bit satisfies \\texttt{answer = true $\\leftrightarrow$\n  SAT.Satisfiable F}, the decision form made explicit.\n\\item \\texttt{original\\_sat\\_main} (line 184): the proposition\n  \\texttt{SqrtLog.Statement.MainTheorem}\n  (\\clonefile{SqrtLog/StatementCanonical.lean}, line 37), a decision-only\n  target for every width $k \\ge 3$ with the same \\texttt{runtimeBound},\n  stated there as a \\texttt{Prop} ``no inhabitant is supplied here'' and\n  inhabited in \\path{Final.lean}.\n\\end{itemize}\nBeyond these, an identifier search over the family's 3,977 \\texttt{.lean}\nfiles (\\repofile{paper3/scripts/beyond\\_claims.py}, read-only) finds no\noccurrence of \\texttt{SimpleGraph}, \\texttt{PerfectMatching},\n\\texttt{Coloring}, \\texttt{IndependentSet}, \\texttt{CircuitSize},\n\\texttt{OrthogonalVectors}, \\texttt{MaxSat}, \\texttt{TensorNetwork},\n\\texttt{HermiteInterp}, \\texttt{ENP}, \\texttt{SIZE},\n\\texttt{SetSplitting}, \\texttt{GapETH} or \\texttt{NSETH} as an\nidentifier: none of the corollaries, none of Appendix~D, and none of\nthe hypotheses is formalised. The comparator JSON names one theorem,\n\\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat}, and no definition\n(\\clonefile{lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.json}). A grep is\nnecessary and not sufficient (Section~\\ref{sec:proof}); here it is used\nfor absence, where a false negative would require a corollary to be\nformalised under none of the words its own statement uses.\n\n\\subsection{The table}\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\caption{Every claim of the manuscript examined in this section: where it\nis stated (page of \\texttt{paper.pdf}, lines of the committed extract),\nits status in the sense of Section~\\ref{sec:beyond-statuses}, and the\none-line reason. Generated, with every page and fragment checked against\nthe extract and the clone, by \\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/beyond\\_claims.py}.}\n\\label{tab:beyond-claims}\n\\scriptsize\n\\input{tables/beyond_claims}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\\label{sec:beyond-not}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{No judgement on the paper-only proofs.} Appendix~D,\n  Appendix~C.1 and the two sharper bounds of Theorem~1.1 were located\n  and quoted, not read for correctness. ``Paper-only'' means not in the\n  Lean library at commit \\texttt{4eaee875}; it does not mean wrong, and\n  nothing here suggests it.\n\\item \\textbf{No judgement on the supported arguments either.} The\n  reductions behind Corollaries~1.2, 1.3, C.1, C.2 and the Set Splitting\n  transfer are standard in kind and short, and we checked only which\n  property of the counter they use. Whether each is correct as written\n  is the manuscript's, not ours.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about what the verified exponent would yield\n  through Corollary~1.4's argument.} A reader can rerun Appendix~C.1\n  with $a(t)$ replaced by the verified rate; we did not, because it is\n  a question about a paper-only proof.\n\\item \\textbf{No constants.} Every $O_k(1)$ and $O_{k,D}(1)$ in this\n  section is as unknown as the comparator's $C, A, S, d, e$; the\n  comparisons of Table~\\ref{tab:beyond-exponents} are at constant $1$\n  and say nothing about any machine's running time.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing beyond the collection.} Whether the sharper bounds\n  have since been formalised elsewhere, or the manuscript revised, is\n  not examined; the commit and the dated manuscript are what is read.\n\\end{itemize}\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4188,"cache_read_input_tokens":208322,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4188,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"43cbaf52-3109-48ae-9853-92f13356d98c","uuid":"26e0dcbd-2e2e-4578-8b16-1b014ea62fc9","timestamp":"2026-11-05T03:00:34.736Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj2p1pJ69q4QuwvU8cYX","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_017Qjz7a44pxssUSpmCvGnb3":{"file_path":"/home/al/dev/MOSP/paper3/latex/sec3_beyond.tex","content":"% What the manuscript claims beyond the verified statement -- drafted by loop0015\n% item 03 (2026-11-05). Every manuscript quotation gives the page of paper.pdf and\n% the line of reports/scratch/eth_audit/paper.txt (page = form feeds before the\n% line + 1); every Lean location is in the clone at commit 4eaee875, read with\n% cat/sed -n only. Every page, line, fragment and number of this section is\n% checked and the two tables are written by\n%   python3 -I paper3/scripts/beyond_claims.py\n% which exits non-zero if anything has moved.\n\\section{What the manuscript claims beyond the verified statement}\n\\label{sec:beyond}\n\nThe manuscript \\citep{OAI:The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026}\nis 67 pages; the comparator statement is 220 lines. Section~\\ref{sec:statement}\nread the statement. This section reads the manuscript's claims one by one\nand says, for each, whether it is what the Lean theorem asserts, whether it\nfollows from the Lean theorem by an argument that exists only on paper, or\nwhether it rests on a bound that the Lean library does not contain. The\ncollection's own scope note draws the line in two sentences, which we quote\nin full (\\clonefile{lean/docs/378.md}, lines 9 and 11):\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n``For fixed $k \\ge 2$, Lean proves that a deterministic machine exactly\ncounts satisfying assignments of a canonically encoded $k$-CNF on $n$\nvariables in time $(L+1)^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{O_k(n(\\log\\log n)^2/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$,\nwhere $L$ is the encoding's bit length. At every step, the machine has\nvisited at most $(L+1)^{O_k(1)}$ tape cells in total.''\n\n``For $m$ clauses, the October 25 manuscript gives the sharper time bound\n$(n+m)^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{O_k(n\\log\\log n/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$. When $m \\le Dn$ for\nfixed $D > 0$, this improves to\n$(n+m)^{O_{k,D}(1)}\\,2^{O_{k,D}(n/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$.''\n\\end{quote}\nEverything below is an elaboration of that note against the manuscript's\ntext and the library's files. Table~\\ref{tab:beyond-claims} is the summary.\n\n\\subsection{Three statuses, and what ``paper-only'' means}\n\\label{sec:beyond-statuses}\n\nWe tag every claim with one of three words.\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{Verified.} The claim is the comparator statement\n  \\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat}, or one of its conjuncts, or a theorem of\n  the solution tree that the replay of Section~\\ref{sec:replay} compiled\n  and whose axioms it printed. Nothing else is called verified.\n\\item \\textbf{Supported.} The claim follows from the verified statement by\n  an argument that is on paper only: a reduction of a few lines, or a\n  citation. The argument is not in the Lean library, and we have not\n  checked it; what we have checked is that the only property of the\n  counter it uses is one the verified statement provides, and that the\n  exponent it inherits is therefore $\\rhon$, not a sharper one.\n\\item \\textbf{Paper-only.} The claim rests on a bound that the Lean\n  library at commit \\texttt{4eaee875} does not contain: Theorem~1.1's two\n  sharper exponents, and whatever is derived from them.\n\\end{itemize}\n\nThe brief for this section had two tags, verified and paper-only; the\nmiddle one is ours, because the manuscript's corollaries are of two\ndifferent kinds and a single word would have put Corollary~1.3 (a\ncounting-preserving encoding into the verified counter) beside\nCorollary~1.4 (a bound whose proof calls the fixed-density exponent by\nname). In the brief's partition, ``supported'' is a sub-case of\n``paper-only'': not in the library. In no case does ``paper-only'' mean\n``wrong''. We did not read the manuscript's proofs for correctness; the\ntag records where a claim is established, not whether.\n\n\\subsection{The sharper exponents of Theorem~1.1}\n\\label{sec:beyond-exponents}\n\nTheorem~1.1 (p.~2, lines 56--62) states two running-time bounds for the\nsame deterministic algorithm: $(n+m)^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{O_k(n\\log\\log n/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$\nfor every $k$-CNF on $n \\ge 20$ declared variables, and\n$(n+m)^{O_{k,D}(1)}\\,2^{O_{k,D}(n/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$ when $m \\le Dn$ for a\nfixed $D > 0$; both with $(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$ bits of space (line 60). The\nsentence that follows the theorem is the one the title of this paper is\nabout (p.~2, lines 63--67):\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n``The rational interpolation argument in Section 6.4 gives the weaker\nexponent $O_k(\\rho(n))$, where\n$\\rho(n) = n(\\log\\log n)^2/\\sqrt{\\log n} = n/(\\log n)^{1/2+o(1)}$.\nAppendix D proves both bounds in Theorem 1.1 by tensor contraction.''\n\\end{quote}\nProposition~6.4 (p.~43, lines 2474--2476) is the weaker bound as a\nstatement of its own: for each fixed $k \\ge 2$, the interpolation\nalgorithm counts in $(n+m)^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{O_k(\\rho(n))}$ bit operations and\n$(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$ bits of space, for $n \\ge 20$.\n\n\\paragraph{Which of the three is in the Lean.}\nThe comparator's \\runtimeScale{} (lines 132--134 of the comparator file)\nis $n(\\ln\\ln(n+16))^2/\\sqrt{\\ln(n+16)}$, the squared form. The solution's\nown base-2 exponent \\texttt{rho}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Asymptotics.lean}, lines 10--11) is\n$n(\\log_2\\log_2 n)^2/\\sqrt{\\log_2 n}$, Proposition~6.4's $\\rho$ with the\nbase made explicit, and \\texttt{runtimeScale\\_le\\_four\\_rho} (lines\n190--191) bounds the comparator's exponent by four times it for\n$n \\ge 20$. The solution's sparsification parameters are Proposition~6.4's\nto the letter: the proof selects $\\lambda_n = \\lceil\\log_2 n\\rceil$,\n$\\nu_n = \\lceil\\log_2\\lambda_n\\rceil$ and\n$s = \\lfloor\\sqrt{\\lambda_n}/\\nu_n^2\\rfloor$, ``the largest nonnegative\ninteger satisfying $s^2\\nu_n^4 \\le \\lambda_n$'' (p.~43, lines 2480--2486),\nand \\path{Asymptotics.lean} lines 14--20 define \\texttt{sparsifyLambda},\n\\texttt{sparsifyNu} and \\texttt{sparsifySchedule} as exactly those three\nquantities in natural-number arithmetic. So the verified exponent is the\nweaker one, and the verified proof is organised around Section~6.4's\nparameters; what Appendix~D proves is not in the library in any form we\ncould find (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond-lean}). The general exponent\n$n\\log\\log n/\\sqrt{\\log n}$ and the fixed-density exponent\n$n/\\sqrt{\\log n}$ are \\textbf{paper-only}: proved at p.~57 (lines\n3222--3246) and p.~54 (lines 3045--3064) respectively, by the contraction\ntheorem of Appendix~D.\n\n\\paragraph{Both bounds from one algorithm.}\nThe manuscript notes that a single algorithm attains both paper-only\nbounds, switching on $m \\le n\\nu$ (p.~57, lines 3247--3250). That\nalgorithm is not the verified one either.\n\n\\paragraph{The three exponents, in numbers.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:beyond-exponents} puts the three exponents side by side,\neach divided by $n$ and each at constant $1$. The verified ratio is the\none of Table~\\ref{tab:runtime-scale}. The general paper-only ratio,\n$\\ln\\ln n/\\sqrt{\\ln n}$, never reaches $1$: it peaks at $2/e = 0.736$\nwhere $\\ln n = e^2$, that is $n = e^{e^2} \\approx 1{,}618$, and falls on\nboth sides. The fixed-density ratio $1/\\sqrt{\\ln n}$ decreases from\n$0.578$ at $n = 20$. So, at constant $1$, both paper-only guarantees sit\nbelow $2^n$ at every size, and the verified one sits above $2^n$ from\n$n = 50$ to $n \\approx 10^{2390}$. That is the whole content of the\ntitle's qualifier ``verified'': the manuscript's exponents would make a\ndifferent sentence, and they are not what the kernel checked. Two\ncautions apply to the table equally. The constants in front of every\nexponent are $O_k(1)$ or $O_{k,D}(1)$ and are not computed anywhere, in\nthe manuscript or in the Lean; and the manuscript's exponents are\nundefined below $n = e$ and are stated for $n \\ge 20$, while the\nverified one is stated for all $n$ (the $+16$). Figure~\\ref{fig:central}\ndraws the same three curves.\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\caption{The three exponents of the manuscript divided by $n$, at constant\n$1$: the verified $\\rhon/n = (\\ln\\ln(n+16))^2/\\sqrt{\\ln(n+16)}$\n(comparator lines 132--134); Theorem~1.1's general exponent\n$\\ln\\ln n/\\sqrt{\\ln n}$ (p.~2, line 59); its fixed-density exponent\n$1/\\sqrt{\\ln n}$ (p.~2, line 62). Natural logarithms throughout (p.~1,\nlines 48--49). The row $e^{e^2}$ is the peak of the general ratio\n($2/e$). Regenerate: \\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/beyond\\_claims.py}.}\n\\label{tab:beyond-exponents}\n\\small\n\\input{tables/beyond_exponents}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{The abstract's form.}\nThe abstract states the running time as\n$(n+m)^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{n/(\\log n)^{1/2+o(1)}}$ (p.~1, lines 6--9). Since\n$(\\ln\\ln n)^2 = (\\ln n)^{o(1)}$, the verified exponent is of that form\ntoo, and the manuscript says so at lines 64--66. We tag the abstract's\nform \\textbf{verified}, with the remark that its $o(1)$ absorbs exactly\nthe factor the three rows of Table~\\ref{tab:beyond-exponents} differ by:\nat $n = 10^6$ it is $(\\ln\\ln n)^2 = 6.9$ for the verified exponent,\n$\\ln\\ln n = 2.6$ for the general one and $1$ for the fixed-density one.\n\n\\subsection{The corollaries}\n\\label{sec:beyond-corollaries}\n\n\\paragraph{Finding and sampling a satisfying assignment (p.~2, lines 68--71).}\n``When $\\#F > 0$, the standard self-reduction finds a satisfying\nassignment by fixing one variable at a time and checking that the\nremaining count is positive. Choosing each bit according to the two\nconditional counts instead gives an exactly uniform random satisfying\nassignment,'' with polynomial expected overhead and a citation.\n\\textbf{Supported}: the self-reduction calls the verified counter at\nmost $n$ times on formulas of the same width, and the verified statement\nprovides the exact count it needs. The reduction is two sentences of\nprose and is not in the library.\n\n\\paragraph{Corollary 1.2, Max-$k$-SAT within $\\varepsilon m$ (p.~2, lines 77--94).}\nA randomised algorithm finds an assignment satisfying at least\n$\\mathrm{OPT}(F) - \\varepsilon m$ clauses with probability\n$1 - 2^{-\\Omega(n)}$ in $(n+m)^{O_{k,\\varepsilon}(1)}\\,2^{O_{k,\\varepsilon}(\\rho(n))}$\ntime (line 80). The corollary is stated with $\\rho(n)$ itself, the\nverified exponent. Its proof samples $O(n/\\varepsilon^2)$ clauses,\nbuilds a threshold circuit of size $O_{k,\\varepsilon}(n)$, reduces it to\na 3-CNF with $O_{k,\\varepsilon}(n)$ variables by a cited lemma, and calls\nTheorem~1.1 with binary search and self-reduction (lines 82--94).\n\\textbf{Supported}: the exponent matches the verified one because\n$\\rho(cn) = O_c(\\rho(n))$, and every call is to a fixed-width counter\non $O(n)$ variables; the sampling, the circuit and the reduction are on\npaper. Its randomness is in the reduction, not in the counter.\n\n\\paragraph{Corollary 1.3, independent sets, colourings, perfect matchings (p.~2--3, lines 100--120).}\nThe number of independent sets and, for fixed $q$, of proper\n$q$-colourings of a graph on $v$ vertices and $e$ edges in $2^{o(v)}$\ntime, and the number of perfect matchings in $2^{o(v+e)}$ time, all in\npolynomial space. The encodings are explicit: one variable per vertex\nand a 2-clause per edge (lines 107--112); $q$ variables per vertex with\nexactly-one and no-equal-colour clauses, a $q$-CNF on $qv$ variables\n(lines 112--115); one variable per edge and a linear-size circuit for\n``exactly one selected edge meets each vertex'', reduced to 3-CNF with\n$O(v+e)$ variables, whose gate variables are determined by the edge\nbits so that the count is preserved (lines 116--119).\n\\textbf{Supported}: the conclusion is $2^{o(\\cdot)}$, which needs only\n$\\rhon = o(n)$ \\leanref{rho-little-o}, and each encoding is\ncounting-preserving into a fixed-width CNF on $O(v)$ or $O(v+e)$\nvariables. None of the three encodings is in the library.\n\n\\paragraph{Corollary 1.4, a circuit lower bound for $\\mathrm{E}^{\\mathrm{NP}}$ (p.~3, lines 121--127).}\n``The fixed-density case of Theorem 1.1 gives a circuit lower bound for\n$\\mathrm{E}^{\\mathrm{NP}}$'' (line 121): a language $L \\in \\mathrm{E}^{\\mathrm{NP}}$\nand a constant $c > 0$ with $C_L(n) > c\\,n\\sqrt{\\log n}$ for infinitely\nmany $n$, where $C_L(n)$ counts non-input fan-in-two gates (lines\n123--127). The proof is Appendix~C.1 (p.~51--52, lines 2925--2967),\nWilliams's witness-circuit argument with a local reduction, and it\ninvokes the paper-only exponent in so many words: ``Put\n$a(t) = \\sqrt{\\log_2 t}$. Testing whether the count in the\nconstant-density part of Theorem 1.1 is positive decides 3-SAT with $V$\nvariables and at most $5V$ clauses in time $V^b\\,2^{AV/a(V)}$ for fixed\n$A, b > 0$'' (p.~52, lines 2949--2950); the threshold\n$s_n = \\lfloor\\eta n\\lfloor a(n)\\rfloor\\rfloor$ is chosen against that\n$a$ (line 2953), and the constant $c$ comes out as\n$\\sqrt{\\eta/(d\\log 2)}$ (line 2967). \\textbf{Paper-only}: the\nquantitative bound $c\\,n\\sqrt{\\log n}$ is tied to $2^{AV/\\sqrt{\\log V}}$,\nwhich the library does not contain. What the same argument would give\nfrom the verified exponent is a question about the manuscript's proof,\nwhich we do not examine (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond-not}).\n\n\\paragraph{Corollary C.1, $\\mathrm{E}^{\\mathrm{NP}} \\not\\subseteq \\mathrm{SIZE}(O(n))$ (p.~51, lines 2912--2916).}\n``Williams proved that if ETH is false, then $\\mathrm{E}^{\\mathrm{NP}}$\ndoes not have linear-size circuits [Wil13, Theorem 6.1]. Apply this\nimplication to the deterministic subexponential-time 3-SAT algorithm\nsupplied by Theorem 1.1.'' \\textbf{Supported}: the only input is a\ndeterministic $2^{o(n)}$ 3-SAT decision, which the verified statement\nprovides on its answer tape for $k = 3$; the implication is a citation.\nThe manuscript's own scope sentence applies: the corollary ``does not\nassert a superpolynomial circuit lower bound for NP'' (lines 2923--2924).\n\n\\paragraph{Corollary C.2, inverting constant-locality maps (p.~52, lines 2974--2977).}\nFor a Boolean map whose every output bit reads at most $d$ input bits,\nthe preimage count $|G^{-1}(\\vec b)|$ and a preimage in deterministic\ntime $(n+m)^{O_d(1)}\\,2^{O_d(\\rho(n))}$ and polynomial space, for\n$n \\ge 20$. Stated with $\\rho(n)$. The proof writes one clause per\nforbidden local assignment, a CNF of width at most $d$ on the original\n$n$ variables with at most $2^d m$ clauses whose satisfying assignments\nare exactly the preimages, and applies Theorem~1.1 with\n$k = \\max\\{2, d\\}$ (lines 2979--2988). \\textbf{Supported}: the\nencoding is on paper; the counter and the exponent are the verified\nones. The remark that follows, on Goldreich's candidate one-way\nfunctions (lines 2994--2999), inherits the same status and the\nmanuscript's own limit, ``This does not rule out one-wayness against\npolynomial-time algorithms'' (line 2999).\n\n\\subsection{Appendix D: the contraction route}\n\\label{sec:beyond-appendix-d}\n\nAppendix~D, ``A sharper bound for exact counting'' (p.~53--64, from line\n3001), proves Theorem~1.1 ``by contracting Boolean tensor networks. The\ntwo running times reflect the number of wires in the networks'' (lines\n3002--3003). Theorem~D.1 (p.~53, lines 3017--3028) is the contraction\ntheorem: a bipartite tensor network with at most $Cs$ edges, maximum\ndegree at most $\\lceil s^{9/10}\\rceil$ and entries in\n$(\\mathbb{Z}/2^{P_0}\\mathbb{Z})[V]/(V^\\ell)$ is contracted in\n$(s+L)^{O(1)}\\,2^{O(s/\\sqrt{\\log s})}$ bit operations and polynomial\nspace. The fixed-density proof (p.~54, lines 3045--3064) encodes a\nformula as a network with $O_k(n+m)$ edges and applies Theorem~D.1 with\n$s = n + m$; the arbitrary-density proof (p.~57, lines 3222--3246)\ncombines the sparsification of Section~6 with Lemma~D.2 (p.~54, line\n3069) and reaches the exponent $O_k(n\\log\\log n/\\sqrt{\\log n})$ at line\n3243. Section~D.4 changes the logarithm convention for its own proof,\n``log denotes a base-two logarithm and ln denotes a natural logarithm''\n(p.~57, lines 3251--3252). All of this is \\textbf{paper-only}: no\ndeclaration about tensor networks, contraction, Hermite recovery or\npackets exists in the family's 3,977 files, by the identifier search of\nSection~\\ref{sec:beyond-lean}. We make no statement about Appendix~D's\nargument; we did not read it for correctness.\n\n\\subsection{The hypotheses of \\S1.1.1 and the fine-grained remarks of \\S1.1.3}\n\\label{sec:beyond-hypotheses}\n\n\\paragraph{\\S1.1.1 (p.~4, lines 187--191).}\n``Theorem 1.1 refutes ETH, SETH, Gap-ETH, and NSETH. Indeed, testing\nwhether the exact count is positive or zero decides both satisfiability\nand unsatisfiability in deterministic $2^{o(n)}(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$ time for\nevery fixed $k$; deciding satisfiability also solves the Gap-ETH promise\nproblem. The deterministic algorithm also refutes the bounded-error\nrandomized versions of ETH [\\ldots] and SETH [\\ldots], and of Gap-ETH.''\nAnd \\S1.1.2 (p.~5, lines 211--212): ``Our algorithm (Theorem 1.1) solves\n\\#3-SAT deterministically in $2^{o(n)}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$ time and\ntherefore refutes \\#ETH.'' \\textbf{Supported}, and by the shortest\nargument in the manuscript: the verified statement is a deterministic\nmachine whose answer tape carries $[0 < \\mathtt{count}\\ F]$ and whose\ncount tape carries the count, within $2^{o(n)}$ times a polynomial, for\neach fixed $k \\ge 2$; each hypothesis is the negation of that. The\nhypotheses themselves are not defined in the library (the strings\n``ETH'' and ``SETH'' occur in the collection's Lean only in two files of\na different family, \\clonefile{lean/OAI/Combinatorics/ParityLifts/},\nwhich take an ETH-like proposition as a hypothesis and are not in the\nclosure). Section~\\ref{sec:consequences} states each hypothesis with its\nquantifiers and says exactly how the verified statement negates it; the\ntwo sentences the manuscript adds at lines 191--194, on unconditional\nlower bounds for restricted proof systems and on Williams's 2016\nMA-SETH protocol, are context, not claims about the result.\n\n\\paragraph{\\S1.1.3 (p.~5, lines 213--243).}\nAfter a list of lower bounds proved from ETH or SETH (lines 213--225),\nthe manuscript writes: ``Our refutation of ETH and SETH removes one basis\nfor the hardness claims above. Some of the same lower bounds follow from\nweaker hypotheses that may still hold'' (lines 226--227), names the\nOrthogonal Vectors Conjecture (lines 229--232) and the hypothesis that\nunrestricted CNF-SAT admits no $2^{(1-\\delta)n}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$\nalgorithm, adding: ``Theorem 1.1 gives a running-time guarantee for each\nfixed clause width $k$, while this hypothesis allows the width to grow\nwith $n$'' (lines 235--236). These are \\textbf{paper-only} remarks in\nthe sense that nothing about any other hypothesis or any reduction is in\nthe library; they are also the manuscript's own statement of what the\nresult does not do, and Section~\\ref{sec:consequences} rests on them.\nThe one positive transfer, Set Splitting with sets of size at most $k$\nby one variable per element and two clauses per set (lines 237--243), is\n\\textbf{supported} in the same sense as Corollary~1.3: an encoding on\npaper into the verified counter, inheriting $\\rhon$. The caveat at\np.~2, lines 95--99, that $2^{o(n)}$ bounds ``do not transfer under\ngeneral polynomial-time reductions,'' is not a claim but a limit the\nmanuscript places on its own result; it is quoted again in\nSection~\\ref{sec:consequences}.\n\n\\subsection{What the library carries beyond the comparator statement}\n\\label{sec:beyond-lean}\n\n``Paper-only'' is defined against the whole library at commit\n\\texttt{4eaee875}, not only against the comparator file, so we say what\nelse the library proves about this family. \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}\n(201 lines) states five theorems, each followed by a \\texttt{\\#print\naxioms} directive (lines 197--201), and the replay's build log carries\nthe kernel's answer for all five: the three standard axioms\n(\\repofile{paper3/replay/logs/run2/axioms\\_in\\_build.txt}).\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\texttt{compiled\\_exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat} (line 10) and\n  \\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat} (line 74): the comparator statement,\n  first over the explicit 80-register, 1,220-tape program and then over\n  the machine it compiles to (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}).\n\\item \\texttt{exact\\_count\\_eth} (lines 104--147): the same conclusion\n  with the polynomial factor written as $(n + |F| + 1)^{2d}$ instead of\n  $(L+1)^d$, the exponent as $4A\\cdot\\mathtt{rho}\\ n$ in base 2, and the\n  proviso $20 \\le n$ (line 109). This is Proposition~6.4's\n  $(n+m)^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{O_k(\\rho(n))}$ form in the library, so the\n  manuscript's polynomial factor is verified as well as its exponent; it\n  is \\textbf{verified}, and it is still the weaker exponent.\n\\item \\texttt{sat\\_polynomial\\_space} (lines 149--183), for $k \\ge 3$:\n  the answer bit satisfies \\texttt{answer = true $\\leftrightarrow$\n  SAT.Satisfiable F}, the decision form made explicit.\n\\item \\texttt{original\\_sat\\_main} (line 184): the proposition\n  \\texttt{SqrtLog.Statement.MainTheorem}\n  (\\clonefile{SqrtLog/StatementCanonical.lean}, line 37), a decision-only\n  target for every width $k \\ge 3$ with the same \\texttt{runtimeBound},\n  stated there as a \\texttt{Prop} ``no inhabitant is supplied here'' and\n  inhabited in \\path{Final.lean}.\n\\end{itemize}\nBeyond these, an identifier search over the family's 3,977 \\texttt{.lean}\nfiles (\\repofile{paper3/scripts/beyond\\_claims.py}, read-only) finds no\noccurrence of \\texttt{SimpleGraph}, \\texttt{PerfectMatching},\n\\texttt{Coloring}, \\texttt{IndependentSet}, \\texttt{CircuitSize},\n\\texttt{OrthogonalVectors}, \\texttt{MaxSat}, \\texttt{TensorNetwork},\n\\texttt{HermiteInterp}, \\texttt{ENP}, \\texttt{SIZE},\n\\texttt{SetSplitting}, \\texttt{GapETH} or \\texttt{NSETH} as an\nidentifier: none of the corollaries, none of Appendix~D, and none of\nthe hypotheses is formalised. The comparator JSON names one theorem,\n\\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat}, and no definition\n(\\clonefile{lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.json}). A grep is\nnecessary and not sufficient (Section~\\ref{sec:proof}); here it is used\nfor absence, where a false negative would require a corollary to be\nformalised under none of the words its own statement uses.\n\n\\subsection{The table}\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\caption{Every claim of the manuscript examined in this section: where it\nis stated (page of \\texttt{paper.pdf}, lines of the committed extract),\nits status in the sense of Section~\\ref{sec:beyond-statuses}, and the\none-line reason. Generated, with every page and fragment checked against\nthe extract and the clone, by \\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/beyond\\_claims.py}.}\n\\label{tab:beyond-claims}\n\\scriptsize\n\\input{tables/beyond_claims}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\\label{sec:beyond-not}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{No judgement on the paper-only proofs.} Appendix~D,\n  Appendix~C.1 and the two sharper bounds of Theorem~1.1 were located\n  and quoted, not read for correctness. ``Paper-only'' means not in the\n  Lean library at commit \\texttt{4eaee875}; it does not mean wrong, and\n  nothing here suggests it.\n\\item \\textbf{No judgement on the supported arguments either.} The\n  reductions behind Corollaries~1.2, 1.3, C.1, C.2 and the Set Splitting\n  transfer are standard in kind and short, and we checked only which\n  property of the counter they use. Whether each is correct as written\n  is the manuscript's, not ours.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about what the verified exponent would yield\n  through Corollary~1.4's argument.} A reader can rerun Appendix~C.1\n  with $a(t)$ replaced by the verified rate; we did not, because it is\n  a question about a paper-only proof.\n\\item \\textbf{No constants.} Every $O_k(1)$ and $O_{k,D}(1)$ in this\n  section is as unknown as the comparator's $C, A, S, d, e$; the\n  comparisons of Table~\\ref{tab:beyond-exponents} are at constant $1$\n  and say nothing about any machine's running time.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing beyond the collection.} Whether the sharper bounds\n  have since been formalised elsewhere, or the manuscript revised, is\n  not examined; the commit and the dated manuscript are what is read.\n\\end{itemize}\n"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017Qjz7a44pxssUSpmCvGnb3","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /home/al/dev/MOSP/paper3/latex/sec3_beyond.tex has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"43cbaf52-3109-48ae-9853-92f13356d98c","uuid":"e57bb253-fa96-4c99-96bc-19cecb538173","timestamp":"2026-11-05T03:00:34.753Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"update","filePath":"/home/al/dev/MOSP/paper3/latex/sec3_beyond.tex","content":"% What the manuscript claims beyond the verified statement -- drafted by loop0015\n% item 03 (2026-11-05). Every manuscript quotation gives the page of paper.pdf and\n% the line of reports/scratch/eth_audit/paper.txt (page = form feeds before the\n% line + 1); every Lean location is in the clone at commit 4eaee875, read with\n% cat/sed -n only. Every page, line, fragment and number of this section is\n% checked and the two tables are written by\n%   python3 -I paper3/scripts/beyond_claims.py\n% which exits non-zero if anything has moved.\n\\section{What the manuscript claims beyond the verified statement}\n\\label{sec:beyond}\n\nThe manuscript \\citep{OAI:The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026}\nis 67 pages; the comparator statement is 220 lines. Section~\\ref{sec:statement}\nread the statement. This section reads the manuscript's claims one by one\nand says, for each, whether it is what the Lean theorem asserts, whether it\nfollows from the Lean theorem by an argument that exists only on paper, or\nwhether it rests on a bound that the Lean library does not contain. The\ncollection's own scope note draws the line in two sentences, which we quote\nin full (\\clonefile{lean/docs/378.md}, lines 9 and 11):\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n``For fixed $k \\ge 2$, Lean proves that a deterministic machine exactly\ncounts satisfying assignments of a canonically encoded $k$-CNF on $n$\nvariables in time $(L+1)^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{O_k(n(\\log\\log n)^2/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$,\nwhere $L$ is the encoding's bit length. At every step, the machine has\nvisited at most $(L+1)^{O_k(1)}$ tape cells in total.''\n\n``For $m$ clauses, the October 25 manuscript gives the sharper time bound\n$(n+m)^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{O_k(n\\log\\log n/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$. When $m \\le Dn$ for\nfixed $D > 0$, this improves to\n$(n+m)^{O_{k,D}(1)}\\,2^{O_{k,D}(n/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$.''\n\\end{quote}\nEverything below is an elaboration of that note against the manuscript's\ntext and the library's files. Table~\\ref{tab:beyond-claims} is the summary.\n\n\\subsection{Three statuses, and what ``paper-only'' means}\n\\label{sec:beyond-statuses}\n\nWe tag every claim with one of three words.\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{Verified.} The claim is the comparator statement\n  \\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat}, or one of its conjuncts, or a theorem of\n  the solution tree that the replay of Section~\\ref{sec:replay} compiled\n  and whose axioms it printed. Nothing else is called verified.\n\\item \\textbf{Supported.} The claim follows from the verified statement by\n  an argument that is on paper only: a reduction of a few lines, or a\n  citation. The argument is not in the Lean library, and we have not\n  checked it; what we have checked is that the only property of the\n  counter it uses is one the verified statement provides, and that the\n  exponent it inherits is therefore $\\rhon$, not a sharper one.\n\\item \\textbf{Paper-only.} The claim rests on a bound that the Lean\n  library at commit \\texttt{4eaee875} does not contain: Theorem~1.1's two\n  sharper exponents, and whatever is derived from them.\n\\end{itemize}\n\nThe brief for this section had two tags, verified and paper-only; the\nmiddle one is ours, because the manuscript's corollaries are of two\ndifferent kinds and a single word would have put Corollary~1.3 (a\ncounting-preserving encoding into the verified counter) beside\nCorollary~1.4 (a bound whose proof calls the fixed-density exponent by\nname). In the brief's partition, ``supported'' is a sub-case of\n``paper-only'': not in the library. In no case does ``paper-only'' mean\n``wrong''. We did not read the manuscript's proofs for correctness; the\ntag records where a claim is established, not whether.\n\n\\subsection{The sharper exponents of Theorem~1.1}\n\\label{sec:beyond-exponents}\n\nTheorem~1.1 (p.~2, lines 56--62) states two running-time bounds for the\nsame deterministic algorithm: $(n+m)^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{O_k(n\\log\\log n/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$\nfor every $k$-CNF on $n \\ge 20$ declared variables, and\n$(n+m)^{O_{k,D}(1)}\\,2^{O_{k,D}(n/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$ when $m \\le Dn$ for a\nfixed $D > 0$; both with $(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$ bits of space (line 60). The\nsentence that follows the theorem is the one the title of this paper is\nabout (p.~2, lines 63--67):\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n``The rational interpolation argument in Section 6.4 gives the weaker\nexponent $O_k(\\rho(n))$, where\n$\\rho(n) = n(\\log\\log n)^2/\\sqrt{\\log n} = n/(\\log n)^{1/2+o(1)}$.\nAppendix D proves both bounds in Theorem 1.1 by tensor contraction.''\n\\end{quote}\nProposition~6.4 (p.~43, lines 2474--2476) is the weaker bound as a\nstatement of its own: for each fixed $k \\ge 2$, the interpolation\nalgorithm counts in $(n+m)^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{O_k(\\rho(n))}$ bit operations and\n$(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$ bits of space, for $n \\ge 20$.\n\n\\paragraph{Which of the three is in the Lean.}\nThe comparator's \\runtimeScale{} (lines 132--134 of the comparator file)\nis $n(\\ln\\ln(n+16))^2/\\sqrt{\\ln(n+16)}$, the squared form. The solution's\nown base-2 exponent \\texttt{rho}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Asymptotics.lean}, lines 10--11) is\n$n(\\log_2\\log_2 n)^2/\\sqrt{\\log_2 n}$, Proposition~6.4's $\\rho$ with the\nbase made explicit, and \\texttt{runtimeScale\\_le\\_four\\_rho} (lines\n190--191) bounds the comparator's exponent by four times it for\n$n \\ge 20$. The solution's sparsification parameters are Proposition~6.4's\nto the letter: the proof selects $\\lambda_n = \\lceil\\log_2 n\\rceil$,\n$\\nu_n = \\lceil\\log_2\\lambda_n\\rceil$ and\n$s = \\lfloor\\sqrt{\\lambda_n}/\\nu_n^2\\rfloor$, ``the largest nonnegative\ninteger satisfying $s^2\\nu_n^4 \\le \\lambda_n$'' (p.~43, lines 2480--2486),\nand \\path{Asymptotics.lean} lines 14--20 define \\texttt{sparsifyLambda},\n\\texttt{sparsifyNu} and \\texttt{sparsifySchedule} as exactly those three\nquantities in natural-number arithmetic. So the verified exponent is the\nweaker one, and the verified proof is organised around Section~6.4's\nparameters; what Appendix~D proves is not in the library in any form we\ncould find (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond-lean}). The general exponent\n$n\\log\\log n/\\sqrt{\\log n}$ and the fixed-density exponent\n$n/\\sqrt{\\log n}$ are \\textbf{paper-only}: proved at p.~57 (lines\n3222--3246) and p.~54 (lines 3045--3064) respectively, by the contraction\ntheorem of Appendix~D.\n\n\\paragraph{Both bounds from one algorithm.}\nThe manuscript notes that a single algorithm attains both paper-only\nbounds, switching on $m \\le n\\nu$ (p.~57, lines 3247--3250). That\nalgorithm is not the verified one either.\n\n\\paragraph{The three exponents, in numbers.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:beyond-exponents} puts the three exponents side by side,\neach divided by $n$ and each at constant $1$. The verified ratio is the\none of Table~\\ref{tab:runtime-scale}. The general paper-only ratio,\n$\\ln\\ln n/\\sqrt{\\ln n}$, never reaches $1$: it peaks at $2/e = 0.736$\nwhere $\\ln n = e^2$, that is $n = e^{e^2} \\approx 1{,}618$, and falls on\nboth sides. The fixed-density ratio $1/\\sqrt{\\ln n}$ decreases from\n$0.578$ at $n = 20$. So, at constant $1$, both paper-only guarantees sit\nbelow $2^n$ at every size, and the verified one sits above $2^n$ from\n$n = 50$ to $n \\approx 10^{2390}$. That is the whole content of the\ntitle's qualifier ``verified'': the manuscript's exponents would make a\ndifferent sentence, and they are not what the kernel checked. Two\ncautions apply to the table equally. The constants in front of every\nexponent are $O_k(1)$ or $O_{k,D}(1)$ and are not computed anywhere, in\nthe manuscript or in the Lean; and the manuscript's exponents are\nundefined below $n = e$ and are stated for $n \\ge 20$, while the\nverified one is stated for all $n$ (the $+16$). Figure~\\ref{fig:central}\ndraws the same three curves.\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\caption{The three exponents of the manuscript divided by $n$, at constant\n$1$: the verified $\\rhon/n = (\\ln\\ln(n+16))^2/\\sqrt{\\ln(n+16)}$\n(comparator lines 132--134); Theorem~1.1's general exponent\n$\\ln\\ln n/\\sqrt{\\ln n}$ (p.~2, line 59); its fixed-density exponent\n$1/\\sqrt{\\ln n}$ (p.~2, line 62). Natural logarithms throughout (p.~1,\nlines 48--49). The row $e^{e^2}$ is the peak of the general ratio\n($2/e$). Regenerate: \\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/beyond\\_claims.py}.}\n\\label{tab:beyond-exponents}\n\\small\n\\input{tables/beyond_exponents}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{The abstract's form.}\nThe abstract states the running time as\n$(n+m)^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{n/(\\log n)^{1/2+o(1)}}$ (p.~1, lines 6--9). Since\n$(\\ln\\ln n)^2 = (\\ln n)^{o(1)}$, the verified exponent is of that form\ntoo, and the manuscript says so at lines 64--66. We tag the abstract's\nform \\textbf{verified}, with the remark that its $o(1)$ absorbs exactly\nthe factor the three rows of Table~\\ref{tab:beyond-exponents} differ by:\nat $n = 10^6$ it is $(\\ln\\ln n)^2 = 6.9$ for the verified exponent,\n$\\ln\\ln n = 2.6$ for the general one and $1$ for the fixed-density one.\n\n\\subsection{The corollaries}\n\\label{sec:beyond-corollaries}\n\n\\paragraph{Finding and sampling a satisfying assignment (p.~2, lines 68--71).}\n``When $\\#F > 0$, the standard self-reduction finds a satisfying\nassignment by fixing one variable at a time and checking that the\nremaining count is positive. Choosing each bit according to the two\nconditional counts instead gives an exactly uniform random satisfying\nassignment,'' with polynomial expected overhead and a citation.\n\\textbf{Supported}: the self-reduction calls the verified counter at\nmost $n$ times on formulas of the same width, and the verified statement\nprovides the exact count it needs. The reduction is two sentences of\nprose and is not in the library.\n\n\\paragraph{Corollary 1.2, Max-$k$-SAT within $\\varepsilon m$ (p.~2, lines 77--94).}\nA randomised algorithm finds an assignment satisfying at least\n$\\mathrm{OPT}(F) - \\varepsilon m$ clauses with probability\n$1 - 2^{-\\Omega(n)}$ in $(n+m)^{O_{k,\\varepsilon}(1)}\\,2^{O_{k,\\varepsilon}(\\rho(n))}$\ntime (line 80). The corollary is stated with $\\rho(n)$ itself, the\nverified exponent. Its proof samples $O(n/\\varepsilon^2)$ clauses,\nbuilds a threshold circuit of size $O_{k,\\varepsilon}(n)$, reduces it to\na 3-CNF with $O_{k,\\varepsilon}(n)$ variables by a cited lemma, and calls\nTheorem~1.1 with binary search and self-reduction (lines 82--94).\n\\textbf{Supported}: the exponent matches the verified one because\n$\\rho(cn) = O_c(\\rho(n))$, and every call is to a fixed-width counter\non $O(n)$ variables; the sampling, the circuit and the reduction are on\npaper. Its randomness is in the reduction, not in the counter.\n\n\\paragraph{Corollary 1.3, independent sets, colourings, perfect matchings (p.~2--3, lines 100--120).}\nThe number of independent sets and, for fixed $q$, of proper\n$q$-colourings of a graph on $v$ vertices and $e$ edges in $2^{o(v)}$\ntime, and the number of perfect matchings in $2^{o(v+e)}$ time, all in\npolynomial space. The encodings are explicit: one variable per vertex\nand a 2-clause per edge (lines 107--112); $q$ variables per vertex with\nexactly-one and no-equal-colour clauses, a $q$-CNF on $qv$ variables\n(lines 112--115); one variable per edge and a linear-size circuit for\n``exactly one selected edge meets each vertex'', reduced to 3-CNF with\n$O(v+e)$ variables, whose gate variables are determined by the edge\nbits so that the count is preserved (lines 116--119).\n\\textbf{Supported}: the conclusion is $2^{o(\\cdot)}$, which needs only\n$\\rhon = o(n)$ \\leanref{rho-little-o}, and each encoding is\ncounting-preserving into a fixed-width CNF on $O(v)$ or $O(v+e)$\nvariables. None of the three encodings is in the library.\n\n\\paragraph{Corollary 1.4, a circuit lower bound for $\\mathrm{E}^{\\mathrm{NP}}$ (p.~3, lines 121--127).}\n``The fixed-density case of Theorem 1.1 gives a circuit lower bound for\n$\\mathrm{E}^{\\mathrm{NP}}$'' (line 121): a language $L \\in \\mathrm{E}^{\\mathrm{NP}}$\nand a constant $c > 0$ with $C_L(n) > c\\,n\\sqrt{\\log n}$ for infinitely\nmany $n$, where $C_L(n)$ counts non-input fan-in-two gates (lines\n123--127). The proof is Appendix~C.1 (p.~51--52, lines 2925--2967),\nWilliams's witness-circuit argument with a local reduction, and it\ninvokes the paper-only exponent in so many words: ``Put\n$a(t) = \\sqrt{\\log_2 t}$. Testing whether the count in the\nconstant-density part of Theorem 1.1 is positive decides 3-SAT with $V$\nvariables and at most $5V$ clauses in time $V^b\\,2^{AV/a(V)}$ for fixed\n$A, b > 0$'' (p.~52, lines 2949--2950); the threshold\n$s_n = \\lfloor\\eta n\\lfloor a(n)\\rfloor\\rfloor$ is chosen against that\n$a$ (line 2953), and the constant $c$ comes out as\n$\\sqrt{\\eta/(d\\log 2)}$ (line 2967). \\textbf{Paper-only}: the\nquantitative bound $c\\,n\\sqrt{\\log n}$ is tied to $2^{AV/\\sqrt{\\log V}}$,\nwhich the library does not contain. What the same argument would give\nfrom the verified exponent is a question about the manuscript's proof,\nwhich we do not examine (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond-not}).\n\n\\paragraph{Corollary C.1, $\\mathrm{E}^{\\mathrm{NP}} \\not\\subseteq \\mathrm{SIZE}(O(n))$ (p.~51, lines 2912--2916).}\n``Williams proved that if ETH is false, then $\\mathrm{E}^{\\mathrm{NP}}$\ndoes not have linear-size circuits [Wil13, Theorem 6.1]. Apply this\nimplication to the deterministic subexponential-time 3-SAT algorithm\nsupplied by Theorem 1.1.'' \\textbf{Supported}: the only input is a\ndeterministic $2^{o(n)}$ 3-SAT decision, which the verified statement\nprovides on its answer tape for $k = 3$; the implication is a citation.\nThe manuscript's own scope sentence applies: the corollary ``does not\nassert a superpolynomial circuit lower bound for NP'' (lines 2923--2924).\n\n\\paragraph{Corollary C.2, inverting constant-locality maps (p.~52, lines 2974--2977).}\nFor a Boolean map whose every output bit reads at most $d$ input bits,\nthe preimage count $|G^{-1}(\\vec b)|$ and a preimage in deterministic\ntime $(n+m)^{O_d(1)}\\,2^{O_d(\\rho(n))}$ and polynomial space, for\n$n \\ge 20$. Stated with $\\rho(n)$. The proof writes one clause per\nforbidden local assignment, a CNF of width at most $d$ on the original\n$n$ variables with at most $2^d m$ clauses whose satisfying assignments\nare exactly the preimages, and applies Theorem~1.1 with\n$k = \\max\\{2, d\\}$ (lines 2979--2988). \\textbf{Supported}: the\nencoding is on paper; the counter and the exponent are the verified\nones. The remark that follows, on Goldreich's candidate one-way\nfunctions (lines 2994--2999), inherits the same status and the\nmanuscript's own limit, ``This does not rule out one-wayness against\npolynomial-time algorithms'' (line 2999).\n\n\\subsection{Appendix D: the contraction route}\n\\label{sec:beyond-appendix-d}\n\nAppendix~D, ``A sharper bound for exact counting'' (p.~53--64, from line\n3001), proves Theorem~1.1 ``by contracting Boolean tensor networks. The\ntwo running times reflect the number of wires in the networks'' (lines\n3002--3003). Theorem~D.1 (p.~53, lines 3017--3028) is the contraction\ntheorem: a bipartite tensor network with at most $Cs$ edges, maximum\ndegree at most $\\lceil s^{9/10}\\rceil$ and entries in\n$(\\mathbb{Z}/2^{P_0}\\mathbb{Z})[V]/(V^\\ell)$ is contracted in\n$(s+L)^{O(1)}\\,2^{O(s/\\sqrt{\\log s})}$ bit operations and polynomial\nspace. The fixed-density proof (p.~54, lines 3045--3064) encodes a\nformula as a network with $O_k(n+m)$ edges and applies Theorem~D.1 with\n$s = n + m$; the arbitrary-density proof (p.~57, lines 3222--3246)\ncombines the sparsification of Section~6 with Lemma~D.2 (p.~54, line\n3069) and reaches the exponent $O_k(n\\log\\log n/\\sqrt{\\log n})$ at line\n3243. Section~D.4 changes the logarithm convention for its own proof,\n``log denotes a base-two logarithm and ln denotes a natural logarithm''\n(p.~57, lines 3251--3252). All of this is \\textbf{paper-only}: no\ndeclaration about tensor networks, contraction, Hermite recovery or\npackets exists in the family's 3,977 files, by the identifier search of\nSection~\\ref{sec:beyond-lean}. We make no statement about Appendix~D's\nargument; we did not read it for correctness.\n\n\\subsection{The hypotheses of \\S1.1.1 and the fine-grained remarks of \\S1.1.3}\n\\label{sec:beyond-hypotheses}\n\n\\paragraph{\\S1.1.1 (p.~4, lines 187--191).}\n``Theorem 1.1 refutes ETH, SETH, Gap-ETH, and NSETH. Indeed, testing\nwhether the exact count is positive or zero decides both satisfiability\nand unsatisfiability in deterministic $2^{o(n)}(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$ time for\nevery fixed $k$; deciding satisfiability also solves the Gap-ETH promise\nproblem. The deterministic algorithm also refutes the bounded-error\nrandomized versions of ETH [\\ldots] and SETH [\\ldots], and of Gap-ETH.''\nAnd \\S1.1.2 (p.~5, lines 211--212): ``Our algorithm (Theorem 1.1) solves\n\\#3-SAT deterministically in $2^{o(n)}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$ time and\ntherefore refutes \\#ETH.'' \\textbf{Supported}, and by the shortest\nargument in the manuscript: the verified statement is a deterministic\nmachine whose answer tape carries $[0 < \\mathtt{count}\\ F]$ and whose\ncount tape carries the count, within $2^{o(n)}$ times a polynomial, for\neach fixed $k \\ge 2$; each hypothesis is the negation of that. The\nhypotheses themselves are not defined in the library (the strings\n``ETH'' and ``SETH'' occur in the collection's Lean only in two files of\na different family, \\clonefile{lean/OAI/Combinatorics/ParityLifts/},\nwhich take an ETH-like proposition as a hypothesis and are not in the\nclosure). Section~\\ref{sec:consequences} states each hypothesis with its\nquantifiers and says exactly how the verified statement negates it; the\ntwo sentences the manuscript adds at lines 191--194, on unconditional\nlower bounds for restricted proof systems and on Williams's 2016\nMA-SETH protocol, are context, not claims about the result.\n\n\\paragraph{\\S1.1.3 (p.~5, lines 213--243).}\nAfter a list of lower bounds proved from ETH or SETH (lines 213--225),\nthe manuscript writes: ``Our refutation of ETH and SETH removes one basis\nfor the hardness claims above. Some of the same lower bounds follow from\nweaker hypotheses that may still hold'' (lines 226--227), names the\nOrthogonal Vectors Conjecture (lines 229--232) and the hypothesis that\nunrestricted CNF-SAT admits no $2^{(1-\\delta)n}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$\nalgorithm, adding: ``Theorem 1.1 gives a running-time guarantee for each\nfixed clause width $k$, while this hypothesis allows the width to grow\nwith $n$'' (lines 235--236). These are \\textbf{paper-only} remarks in\nthe sense that nothing about any other hypothesis or any reduction is in\nthe library; they are also the manuscript's own statement of what the\nresult does not do, and Section~\\ref{sec:consequences} rests on them.\nThe one positive transfer, Set Splitting with sets of size at most $k$\nby one variable per element and two clauses per set (lines 237--243), is\n\\textbf{supported} in the same sense as Corollary~1.3: an encoding on\npaper into the verified counter, inheriting $\\rhon$. The caveat at\np.~2, lines 95--99, that $2^{o(n)}$ bounds ``do not transfer under\ngeneral polynomial-time reductions,'' is not a claim but a limit the\nmanuscript places on its own result; it is quoted again in\nSection~\\ref{sec:consequences}.\n\n\\subsection{What the library carries beyond the comparator statement}\n\\label{sec:beyond-lean}\n\n``Paper-only'' is defined against the whole library at commit\n\\texttt{4eaee875}, not only against the comparator file, so we say what\nelse the library proves about this family. \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}\n(201 lines) states five theorems, each followed by a \\texttt{\\#print\naxioms} directive (lines 197--201), and the replay's build log carries\nthe kernel's answer for all five: the three standard axioms\n(\\repofile{paper3/replay/logs/run2/axioms\\_in\\_build.txt}).\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\texttt{compiled\\_exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat} (line 10) and\n  \\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat} (line 74): the comparator statement,\n  first over the explicit 80-register, 1,220-tape program and then over\n  the machine it compiles to (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}).\n\\item \\texttt{exact\\_count\\_eth} (lines 104--147): the same conclusion\n  with the polynomial factor written as $(n + |F| + 1)^{2d}$ instead of\n  $(L+1)^d$, the exponent as $4A\\cdot\\mathtt{rho}\\ n$ in base 2, and the\n  proviso $20 \\le n$ (line 109). This is Proposition~6.4's\n  $(n+m)^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{O_k(\\rho(n))}$ form in the library, so the\n  manuscript's polynomial factor is verified as well as its exponent; it\n  is \\textbf{verified}, and it is still the weaker exponent.\n\\item \\texttt{sat\\_polynomial\\_space} (lines 149--183), for $k \\ge 3$:\n  the answer bit satisfies \\texttt{answer = true $\\leftrightarrow$\n  SAT.Satisfiable F}, the decision form made explicit.\n\\item \\texttt{original\\_sat\\_main} (line 184): the proposition\n  \\texttt{SqrtLog.Statement.MainTheorem}\n  (\\clonefile{SqrtLog/StatementCanonical.lean}, line 37), a decision-only\n  target for every width $k \\ge 3$ with the same \\texttt{runtimeBound},\n  stated there as a \\texttt{Prop} ``no inhabitant is supplied here'' and\n  inhabited in \\path{Final.lean}.\n\\end{itemize}\nBeyond these, an identifier search over the family's 3,977 \\texttt{.lean}\nfiles (\\repofile{paper3/scripts/beyond\\_claims.py}, read-only) finds no\noccurrence of \\texttt{SimpleGraph}, \\texttt{PerfectMatching},\n\\texttt{Coloring}, \\texttt{IndependentSet}, \\texttt{CircuitSize},\n\\texttt{OrthogonalVectors}, \\texttt{MaxSat}, \\texttt{TensorNetwork},\n\\texttt{HermiteInterp}, \\texttt{ENP}, \\texttt{SIZE},\n\\texttt{SetSplitting}, \\texttt{GapETH} or \\texttt{NSETH} as an\nidentifier: none of the corollaries, none of Appendix~D, and none of\nthe hypotheses is formalised. The comparator JSON names one theorem,\n\\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat}, and no definition\n(\\clonefile{lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.json}). A grep is\nnecessary and not sufficient (Section~\\ref{sec:proof}); here it is used\nfor absence, where a false negative would require a corollary to be\nformalised under none of the words its own statement uses.\n\n\\subsection{The table}\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\caption{Every claim of the manuscript examined in this section: where it\nis stated (page of \\texttt{paper.pdf}, lines of the committed extract),\nits status in the sense of Section~\\ref{sec:beyond-statuses}, and the\none-line reason. Generated, with every page and fragment checked against\nthe extract and the clone, by \\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/beyond\\_claims.py}.}\n\\label{tab:beyond-claims}\n\\scriptsize\n\\input{tables/beyond_claims}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\\label{sec:beyond-not}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{No judgement on the paper-only proofs.} Appendix~D,\n  Appendix~C.1 and the two sharper bounds of Theorem~1.1 were located\n  and quoted, not read for correctness. ``Paper-only'' means not in the\n  Lean library at commit \\texttt{4eaee875}; it does not mean wrong, and\n  nothing here suggests it.\n\\item \\textbf{No judgement on the supported arguments either.} The\n  reductions behind Corollaries~1.2, 1.3, C.1, C.2 and the Set Splitting\n  transfer are standard in kind and short, and we checked only which\n  property of the counter they use. Whether each is correct as written\n  is the manuscript's, not ours.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about what the verified exponent would yield\n  through Corollary~1.4's argument.} A reader can rerun Appendix~C.1\n  with $a(t)$ replaced by the verified rate; we did not, because it is\n  a question about a paper-only proof.\n\\item \\textbf{No constants.} Every $O_k(1)$ and $O_{k,D}(1)$ in this\n  section is as unknown as the comparator's $C, A, S, d, e$; the\n  comparisons of Table~\\ref{tab:beyond-exponents} are at constant $1$\n  and say nothing about any machine's running time.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing beyond the collection.} Whether the sharper bounds\n  have since been formalised elsewhere, or the manuscript revised, is\n  not examined; the commit and the dated manuscript are what is read.\n\\end{itemize}\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":5,"newStart":1,"newLines":406,"lines":["-% What the manuscript claims beyond the verified statement -- drafted by loop0015 item 03.","+% What the manuscript claims beyond the verified statement -- drafted by loop0015","+% item 03 (2026-11-05). Every manuscript quotation gives the page of paper.pdf and","+% the line of reports/scratch/eth_audit/paper.txt (page = form feeds before the","+% line + 1); every Lean location is in the clone at commit 4eaee875, read with","+% cat/sed -n only. Every page, line, fragment and number of this section is","+% checked and the two tables are written by","+%   python3 -I paper3/scripts/beyond_claims.py","+% which exits non-zero if anything has moved."," \\section{What the manuscript claims beyond the verified statement}"," \\label{sec:beyond}"," ","-\\placeholder{Item 03 drafts this section: Theorem 1.1's two sharper exponents, Corollary 1.4's quantitative circuit bound, Appendix C's consequences, Appendix D, each tagged verified or paper-only with page and line of the extract. Ends with what this does not claim''.}","+The manuscript \\citep{OAI:The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026}","+is 67 pages; the comparator statement is 220 lines. Section~\\ref{sec:statement}","+read the statement. This section reads the manuscript's claims one by one","+and says, for each, whether it is what the Lean theorem asserts, whether it","+follows from the Lean theorem by an argument that exists only on paper, or","+whether it rests on a bound that the Lean library does not contain. The","+collection's own scope note draws the line in two sentences, which we quote","+in full (\\clonefile{lean/docs/378.md}, lines 9 and 11):","+\\begin{quote}\\small","+``For fixed $k \\ge 2$, Lean proves that a deterministic machine exactly","+counts satisfying assignments of a canonically encoded $k$-CNF on $n$","+variables in time $(L+1)^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{O_k(n(\\log\\log n)^2/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$,","+where $L$ is the encoding's bit length. At every step, the machine has","+visited at most $(L+1)^{O_k(1)}$ tape cells in total.''","+","+``For $m$ clauses, the October 25 manuscript gives the sharper time bound","+$(n+m)^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{O_k(n\\log\\log n/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$. When $m \\le Dn$ for","+fixed $D > 0$, this improves to","+$(n+m)^{O_{k,D}(1)}\\,2^{O_{k,D}(n/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$.''","+\\end{quote}","+Everything below is an elaboration of that note against the manuscript's","+text and the library's files. Table~\\ref{tab:beyond-claims} is the summary.","+","+\\subsection{Three statuses, and what ``paper-only'' means}","+\\label{sec:beyond-statuses}","+","+We tag every claim with one of three words.","+","+\\begin{itemize}","+\\item \\textbf{Verified.} The claim is the comparator statement","+  \\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat}, or one of its conjuncts, or a theorem of","+  the solution tree that the replay of Section~\\ref{sec:replay} compiled","+  and whose axioms it printed. Nothing else is called verified.","+\\item \\textbf{Supported.} The claim follows from the verified statement by","+  an argument that is on paper only: a reduction of a few lines, or a","+  citation. The argument is not in the Lean library, and we have not","+  checked it; what we have checked is that the only property of the","+  counter it uses is one the verified statement provides, and that the","+  exponent it inherits is therefore $\\rhon$, not a sharper one.","+\\item \\textbf{Paper-only.} The claim rests on a bound that the Lean","+  library at commit \\texttt{4eaee875} does not contain: Theorem~1.1's two","+  sharper exponents, and whatever is derived from them.","+\\end{itemize}","+","+The brief for this section had two tags, verified and paper-only; the","+middle one is ours, because the manuscript's corollaries are of two","+different kinds and a single word would have put Corollary~1.3 (a","+counting-preserving encoding into the verified counter) beside","+Corollary~1.4 (a bound whose proof calls the fixed-density exponent by","+name). In the brief's partition, ``supported'' is a sub-case of","+``paper-only'': not in the library. In no case does ``paper-only'' mean","+``wrong''. We did not read the manuscript's proofs for correctness; the","+tag records where a claim is established, not whether.","+","+\\subsection{The sharper exponents of Theorem~1.1}","+\\label{sec:beyond-exponents}","+","+Theorem~1.1 (p.~2, lines 56--62) states two running-time bounds for the","+same deterministic algorithm: $(n+m)^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{O_k(n\\log\\log n/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$","+for every $k$-CNF on $n \\ge 20$ declared variables, and","+$(n+m)^{O_{k,D}(1)}\\,2^{O_{k,D}(n/\\sqrt{\\log n})}$ when $m \\le Dn$ for a","+fixed $D > 0$; both with $(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$ bits of space (line 60). The","+sentence that follows the theorem is the one the title of this paper is","+about (p.~2, lines 63--67):","+\\begin{quote}\\small","+``The rational interpolation argument in Section 6.4 gives the weaker","+exponent $O_k(\\rho(n))$, where","+$\\rho(n) = n(\\log\\log n)^2/\\sqrt{\\log n} = n/(\\log n)^{1/2+o(1)}$.","+Appendix D proves both bounds in Theorem 1.1 by tensor contraction.''","+\\end{quote}","+Proposition~6.4 (p.~43, lines 2474--2476) is the weaker bound as a","+statement of its own: for each fixed $k \\ge 2$, the interpolation","+algorithm counts in $(n+m)^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{O_k(\\rho(n))}$ bit operations and","+$(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$ bits of space, for $n \\ge 20$.","+","+\\paragraph{Which of the three is in the Lean.}","+The comparator's \\runtimeScale{} (lines 132--134 of the comparator file)","+is $n(\\ln\\ln(n+16))^2/\\sqrt{\\ln(n+16)}$, the squared form. The solution's","+own base-2 exponent \\texttt{rho}","+(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Asymptotics.lean}, lines 10--11) is","+$n(\\log_2\\log_2 n)^2/\\sqrt{\\log_2 n}$, Proposition~6.4's $\\rho$ with the","+base made explicit, and \\texttt{runtimeScale\\_le\\_four\\_rho} (lines","+190--191) bounds the comparator's exponent by four times it for","+$n \\ge 20$. The solution's sparsification parameters are Proposition~6.4's","+to the letter: the proof selects $\\lambda_n = \\lceil\\log_2 n\\rceil$,","+$\\nu_n = \\lceil\\log_2\\lambda_n\\rceil$ and","+$s = \\lfloor\\sqrt{\\lambda_n}/\\nu_n^2\\rfloor$, ``the largest nonnegative","+integer satisfying $s^2\\nu_n^4 \\le \\lambda_n$'' (p.~43, lines 2480--2486),","+and \\path{Asymptotics.lean} lines 14--20 define \\texttt{sparsifyLambda},","+\\texttt{sparsifyNu} and \\texttt{sparsifySchedule} as exactly those three","+quantities in natural-number arithmetic. So the verified exponent is the","+weaker one, and the verified proof is organised around Section~6.4's","+parameters; what Appendix~D proves is not in the library in any form we","+could find (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond-lean}). The general exponent","+$n\\log\\log n/\\sqrt{\\log n}$ and the fixed-density exponent","+$n/\\sqrt{\\log n}$ are \\textbf{paper-only}: proved at p.~57 (lines","+3222--3246) and p.~54 (lines 3045--3064) respectively, by the contraction","+theorem of Appendix~D.","+","+\\paragraph{Both bounds from one algorithm.}","+The manuscript notes that a single algorithm attains both paper-only","+bounds, switching on $m \\le n\\nu$ (p.~57, lines 3247--3250). That","+algorithm is not the verified one either.","+","+\\paragraph{The three exponents, in numbers.}","+Table~\\ref{tab:beyond-exponents} puts the three exponents side by side,","+each divided by $n$ and each at constant $1$. The verified ratio is the","+one of Table~\\ref{tab:runtime-scale}. The general paper-only ratio,","+$\\ln\\ln n/\\sqrt{\\ln n}$, never reaches $1$: it peaks at $2/e = 0.736$","+where $\\ln n = e^2$, that is $n = e^{e^2} \\approx 1{,}618$, and falls on","+both sides. The fixed-density ratio $1/\\sqrt{\\ln n}$ decreases from","+$0.578$ at $n = 20$. So, at constant $1$, both paper-only guarantees sit","+below $2^n$ at every size, and the verified one sits above $2^n$ from","+$n = 50$ to $n \\approx 10^{2390}$. That is the whole content of the","+title's qualifier ``verified'': the manuscript's exponents would make a","+different sentence, and they are not what the kernel checked. Two","+cautions apply to the table equally. The constants in front of every","+exponent are $O_k(1)$ or $O_{k,D}(1)$ and are not computed anywhere, in","+the manuscript or in the Lean; and the manuscript's exponents are","+undefined below $n = e$ and are stated for $n \\ge 20$, while the","+verified one is stated for all $n$ (the $+16$). Figure~\\ref{fig:central}","+draws the same three curves.","+","+\\begin{table}[htbp]","+\\centering","+\\caption{The three exponents of the manuscript divided by $n$, at constant","+$1$: the verified $\\rhon/n = (\\ln\\ln(n+16))^2/\\sqrt{\\ln(n+16)}$","+(comparator lines 132--134); Theorem~1.1's general exponent","+$\\ln\\ln n/\\sqrt{\\ln n}$ (p.~2, line 59); its fixed-density exponent","+$1/\\sqrt{\\ln n}$ (p.~2, line 62). Natural logarithms throughout (p.~1,","+lines 48--49). The row $e^{e^2}$ is the peak of the general ratio","+($2/e$). Regenerate: \\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/beyond\\_claims.py}.}","+\\label{tab:beyond-exponents}","+\\small","+\\input{tables/beyond_exponents}","+\\end{table}","+","+\\paragraph{The abstract's form.}","+The abstract states the running time as","+$(n+m)^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{n/(\\log n)^{1/2+o(1)}}$ (p.~1, lines 6--9). Since","+$(\\ln\\ln n)^2 = (\\ln n)^{o(1)}$, the verified exponent is of that form","+too, and the manuscript says so at lines 64--66. We tag the abstract's","+form \\textbf{verified}, with the remark that its $o(1)$ absorbs exactly","+the factor the three rows of Table~\\ref{tab:beyond-exponents} differ by:","+at $n = 10^6$ it is $(\\ln\\ln n)^2 = 6.9$ for the verified exponent,","+$\\ln\\ln n = 2.6$ for the general one and $1$ for the fixed-density one.","+","+\\subsection{The corollaries}","+\\label{sec:beyond-corollaries}","+","+\\paragraph{Finding and sampling a satisfying assignment (p.~2, lines 68--71).}","+``When $\\#F > 0$, the standard self-reduction finds a satisfying","+assignment by fixing one variable at a time and checking that the","+remaining count is positive. Choosing each bit according to the two","+conditional counts instead gives an exactly uniform random satisfying","+assignment,'' with polynomial expected overhead and a citation.","+\\textbf{Supported}: the self-reduction calls the verified counter at","+most $n$ times on formulas of the same width, and the verified statement","+provides the exact count it needs. The reduction is two sentences of","+prose and is not in the library.","+","+\\paragraph{Corollary 1.2, Max-$k$-SAT within $\\varepsilon m$ (p.~2, lines 77--94).}","+A randomised algorithm finds an assignment satisfying at least","+$\\mathrm{OPT}(F) - \\varepsilon m$ clauses with probability","+$1 - 2^{-\\Omega(n)}$ in $(n+m)^{O_{k,\\varepsilon}(1)}\\,2^{O_{k,\\varepsilon}(\\rho(n))}$","+time (line 80). The corollary is stated with $\\rho(n)$ itself, the","+verified exponent. Its proof samples $O(n/\\varepsilon^2)$ clauses,","+builds a threshold circuit of size $O_{k,\\varepsilon}(n)$, reduces it to","+a 3-CNF with $O_{k,\\varepsilon}(n)$ variables by a cited lemma, and calls","+Theorem~1.1 with binary search and self-reduction (lines 82--94).","+\\textbf{Supported}: the exponent matches the verified one because","+$\\rho(cn) = O_c(\\rho(n))$, and every call is to a fixed-width counter","+on $O(n)$ variables; the sampling, the circuit and the reduction are on","+paper. Its randomness is in the reduction, not in the counter.","+","+\\paragraph{Corollary 1.3, independent sets, colourings, perfect matchings (p.~2--3, lines 100--120).}","+The number of independent sets and, for fixed $q$, of proper","+$q$-colourings of a graph on $v$ vertices and $e$ edges in $2^{o(v)}$","+time, and the number of perfect matchings in $2^{o(v+e)}$ time, all in","+polynomial space. The encodings are explicit: one variable per vertex","+and a 2-clause per edge (lines 107--112); $q$ variables per vertex with","+exactly-one and no-equal-colour clauses, a $q$-CNF on $qv$ variables","+(lines 112--115); one variable per edge and a linear-size circuit for","+``exactly one selected edge meets each vertex'', reduced to 3-CNF with","+$O(v+e)$ variables, whose gate variables are determined by the edge","+bits so that the count is preserved (lines 116--119).","+\\textbf{Supported}: the conclusion is $2^{o(\\cdot)}$, which needs only","+$\\rhon = o(n)$ \\leanref{rho-little-o}, and each encoding is","+counting-preserving into a fixed-width CNF on $O(v)$ or $O(v+e)$","+variables. None of the three encodings is in the library.","+","+\\paragraph{Corollary 1.4, a circuit lower bound for $\\mathrm{E}^{\\mathrm{NP}}$ (p.~3, lines 121--127).}","+``The fixed-density case of Theorem 1.1 gives a circuit lower bound for","+$\\mathrm{E}^{\\mathrm{NP}}$'' (line 121): a language $L \\in \\mathrm{E}^{\\mathrm{NP}}$","+and a constant $c > 0$ with $C_L(n) > c\\,n\\sqrt{\\log n}$ for infinitely","+many $n$, where $C_L(n)$ counts non-input fan-in-two gates (lines","+123--127). The proof is Appendix~C.1 (p.~51--52, lines 2925--2967),","+Williams's witness-circuit argument with a local reduction, and it","+invokes the paper-only exponent in so many words: ``Put","+$a(t) = \\sqrt{\\log_2 t}$. Testing whether the count in the","+constant-density part of Theorem 1.1 is positive decides 3-SAT with $V$","+variables and at most $5V$ clauses in time $V^b\\,2^{AV/a(V)}$ for fixed","+$A, b > 0$'' (p.~52, lines 2949--2950); the threshold","+$s_n = \\lfloor\\eta n\\lfloor a(n)\\rfloor\\rfloor$ is chosen against that","+$a$ (line 2953), and the constant $c$ comes out as","+$\\sqrt{\\eta/(d\\log 2)}$ (line 2967). \\textbf{Paper-only}: the","+quantitative bound $c\\,n\\sqrt{\\log n}$ is tied to $2^{AV/\\sqrt{\\log V}}$,","+which the library does not contain. What the same argument would give","+from the verified exponent is a question about the manuscript's proof,","+which we do not examine (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond-not}).","+","+\\paragraph{Corollary C.1, $\\mathrm{E}^{\\mathrm{NP}} \\not\\subseteq \\mathrm{SIZE}(O(n))$ (p.~51, lines 2912--2916).}","+``Williams proved that if ETH is false, then $\\mathrm{E}^{\\mathrm{NP}}$","+does not have linear-size circuits [Wil13, Theorem 6.1]. Apply this","+implication to the deterministic subexponential-time 3-SAT algorithm","+supplied by Theorem 1.1.'' \\textbf{Supported}: the only input is a","+deterministic $2^{o(n)}$ 3-SAT decision, which the verified statement","+provides on its answer tape for $k = 3$; the implication is a citation.","+The manuscript's own scope sentence applies: the corollary ``does not","+assert a superpolynomial circuit lower bound for NP'' (lines 2923--2924).","+","+\\paragraph{Corollary C.2, inverting constant-locality maps (p.~52, lines 2974--2977).}","+For a Boolean map whose every output bit reads at most $d$ input bits,","+the preimage count $|G^{-1}(\\vec b)|$ and a preimage in deterministic","+time $(n+m)^{O_d(1)}\\,2^{O_d(\\rho(n))}$ and polynomial space, for","+$n \\ge 20$. Stated with $\\rho(n)$. The proof writes one clause per","+forbidden local assignment, a CNF of width at most $d$ on the original","+$n$ variables with at most $2^d m$ clauses whose satisfying assignments","+are exactly the preimages, and applies Theorem~1.1 with","+$k = \\max\\{2, d\\}$ (lines 2979--2988). \\textbf{Supported}: the","+encoding is on paper; the counter and the exponent are the verified","+ones. The remark that follows, on Goldreich's candidate one-way","+functions (lines 2994--2999), inherits the same status and the","+manuscript's own limit, ``This does not rule out one-wayness against","+polynomial-time algorithms'' (line 2999).","+","+\\subsection{Appendix D: the contraction route}","+\\label{sec:beyond-appendix-d}","+","+Appendix~D, ``A sharper bound for exact counting'' (p.~53--64, from line","+3001), proves Theorem~1.1 ``by contracting Boolean tensor networks. The","+two running times reflect the number of wires in the networks'' (lines","+3002--3003). Theorem~D.1 (p.~53, lines 3017--3028) is the contraction","+theorem: a bipartite tensor network with at most $Cs$ edges, maximum","+degree at most $\\lceil s^{9/10}\\rceil$ and entries in","+$(\\mathbb{Z}/2^{P_0}\\mathbb{Z})[V]/(V^\\ell)$ is contracted in","+$(s+L)^{O(1)}\\,2^{O(s/\\sqrt{\\log s})}$ bit operations and polynomial","+space. The fixed-density proof (p.~54, lines 3045--3064) encodes a","+formula as a network with $O_k(n+m)$ edges and applies Theorem~D.1 with","+$s = n + m$; the arbitrary-density proof (p.~57, lines 3222--3246)","+combines the sparsification of Section~6 with Lemma~D.2 (p.~54, line","+3069) and reaches the exponent $O_k(n\\log\\log n/\\sqrt{\\log n})$ at line","+3243. Section~D.4 changes the logarithm convention for its own proof,","+``log denotes a base-two logarithm and ln denotes a natural logarithm''","+(p.~57, lines 3251--3252). All of this is \\textbf{paper-only}: no","+declaration about tensor networks, contraction, Hermite recovery or","+packets exists in the family's 3,977 files, by the identifier search of","+Section~\\ref{sec:beyond-lean}. We make no statement about Appendix~D's","+argument; we did not read it for correctness.","+","+\\subsection{The hypotheses of \\S1.1.1 and the fine-grained remarks of \\S1.1.3}","+\\label{sec:beyond-hypotheses}","+","+\\paragraph{\\S1.1.1 (p.~4, lines 187--191).}","+``Theorem 1.1 refutes ETH, SETH, Gap-ETH, and NSETH. Indeed, testing","+whether the exact count is positive or zero decides both satisfiability","+and unsatisfiability in deterministic $2^{o(n)}(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$ time for","+every fixed $k$; deciding satisfiability also solves the Gap-ETH promise","+problem. The deterministic algorithm also refutes the bounded-error","+randomized versions of ETH [\\ldots] and SETH [\\ldots], and of Gap-ETH.''","+And \\S1.1.2 (p.~5, lines 211--212): ``Our algorithm (Theorem 1.1) solves","+\\#3-SAT deterministically in $2^{o(n)}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$ time and","+therefore refutes \\#ETH.'' \\textbf{Supported}, and by the shortest","+argument in the manuscript: the verified statement is a deterministic","+machine whose answer tape carries $[0 < \\mathtt{count}\\ F]$ and whose","+count tape carries the count, within $2^{o(n)}$ times a polynomial, for","+each fixed $k \\ge 2$; each hypothesis is the negation of that. The","+hypotheses themselves are not defined in the library (the strings","+``ETH'' and ``SETH'' occur in the collection's Lean only in two files of","+a different family, \\clonefile{lean/OAI/Combinatorics/ParityLifts/},","+which take an ETH-like proposition as a hypothesis and are not in the","+closure). Section~\\ref{sec:consequences} states each hypothesis with its","+quantifiers and says exactly how the verified statement negates it; the","+two sentences the manuscript adds at lines 191--194, on unconditional","+lower bounds for restricted proof systems and on Williams's 2016","+MA-SETH protocol, are context, not claims about the result.","+","+\\paragraph{\\S1.1.3 (p.~5, lines 213--243).}","+After a list of lower bounds proved from ETH or SETH (lines 213--225),","+the manuscript writes: ``Our refutation of ETH and SETH removes one basis","+for the hardness claims above. Some of the same lower bounds follow from","+weaker hypotheses that may still hold'' (lines 226--227), names the","+Orthogonal Vectors Conjecture (lines 229--232) and the hypothesis that","+unrestricted CNF-SAT admits no $2^{(1-\\delta)n}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$","+algorithm, adding: ``Theorem 1.1 gives a running-time guarantee for each","+fixed clause width $k$, while this hypothesis allows the width to grow","+with $n$'' (lines 235--236). These are \\textbf{paper-only} remarks in","+the sense that nothing about any other hypothesis or any reduction is in","+the library; they are also the manuscript's own statement of what the","+result does not do, and Section~\\ref{sec:consequences} rests on them.","+The one positive transfer, Set Splitting with sets of size at most $k$","+by one variable per element and two clauses per set (lines 237--243), is","+\\textbf{supported} in the same sense as Corollary~1.3: an encoding on","+paper into the verified counter, inheriting $\\rhon$. The caveat at","+p.~2, lines 95--99, that $2^{o(n)}$ bounds ``do not transfer under","+general polynomial-time reductions,'' is not a claim but a limit the","+manuscript places on its own result; it is quoted again in","+Section~\\ref{sec:consequences}.","+","+\\subsection{What the library carries beyond the comparator statement}","+\\label{sec:beyond-lean}","+","+``Paper-only'' is defined against the whole library at commit","+\\texttt{4eaee875}, not only against the comparator file, so we say what","+else the library proves about this family. \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}","+(201 lines) states five theorems, each followed by a \\texttt{\\#print","+axioms} directive (lines 197--201), and the replay's build log carries","+the kernel's answer for all five: the three standard axioms","+(\\repofile{paper3/replay/logs/run2/axioms\\_in\\_build.txt}).","+\\begin{itemize}","+\\item \\texttt{compiled\\_exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat} (line 10) and","+  \\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat} (line 74): the comparator statement,","+  first over the explicit 80-register, 1,220-tape program and then over","+  the machine it compiles to (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}).","+\\item \\texttt{exact\\_count\\_eth} (lines 104--147): the same conclusion","+  with the polynomial factor written as $(n + |F| + 1)^{2d}$ instead of","+  $(L+1)^d$, the exponent as $4A\\cdot\\mathtt{rho}\\ n$ in base 2, and the","+  proviso $20 \\le n$ (line 109). This is Proposition~6.4's","+  $(n+m)^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{O_k(\\rho(n))}$ form in the library, so the","+  manuscript's polynomial factor is verified as well as its exponent; it","+  is \\textbf{verified}, and it is still the weaker exponent.","+\\item \\texttt{sat\\_polynomial\\_space} (lines 149--183), for $k \\ge 3$:","+  the answer bit satisfies \\texttt{answer = true $\\leftrightarrow$","+  SAT.Satisfiable F}, the decision form made explicit.","+\\item \\texttt{original\\_sat\\_main} (line 184): the proposition","+  \\texttt{SqrtLog.Statement.MainTheorem}","+  (\\clonefile{SqrtLog/StatementCanonical.lean}, line 37), a decision-only","+  target for every width $k \\ge 3$ with the same \\texttt{runtimeBound},","+  stated there as a \\texttt{Prop} ``no inhabitant is supplied here'' and","+  inhabited in \\path{Final.lean}.","+\\end{itemize}","+Beyond these, an identifier search over the family's 3,977 \\texttt{.lean}","+files (\\repofile{paper3/scripts/beyond\\_claims.py}, read-only) finds no","+occurrence of \\texttt{SimpleGraph}, \\texttt{PerfectMatching},","+\\texttt{Coloring}, \\texttt{IndependentSet}, \\texttt{CircuitSize},","+\\texttt{OrthogonalVectors}, \\texttt{MaxSat}, \\texttt{TensorNetwork},","+\\texttt{HermiteInterp}, \\texttt{ENP}, \\texttt{SIZE},","+\\texttt{SetSplitting}, \\texttt{GapETH} or \\texttt{NSETH} as an","+identifier: none of the corollaries, none of Appendix~D, and none of","+the hypotheses is formalised. The comparator JSON names one theorem,","+\\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat}, and no definition","+(\\clonefile{lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.json}). A grep is","+necessary and not sufficient (Section~\\ref{sec:proof}); here it is used","+for absence, where a false negative would require a corollary to be","+formalised under none of the words its own statement uses.","+","+\\subsection{The table}","+","+\\begin{table}[htbp]","+\\caption{Every claim of the manuscript examined in this section: where it","+is stated (page of \\texttt{paper.pdf}, lines of the committed extract),","+its status in the sense of Section~\\ref{sec:beyond-statuses}, and the","+one-line reason. Generated, with every page and fragment checked against","+the extract and the clone, by \\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/beyond\\_claims.py}.}","+\\label{tab:beyond-claims}","+\\scriptsize","+\\input{tables/beyond_claims}","+\\end{table}","+","+\\subsection{What this does not claim}","+\\label{sec:beyond-not}","+","+\\begin{itemize}","+\\item \\textbf{No judgement on the paper-only proofs.} Appendix~D,","+  Appendix~C.1 and the two sharper bounds of Theorem~1.1 were located","+  and quoted, not read for correctness. ``Paper-only'' means not in the","+  Lean library at commit \\texttt{4eaee875}; it does not mean wrong, and","+  nothing here suggests it.","+\\item \\textbf{No judgement on the supported arguments either.} The","+  reductions behind Corollaries~1.2, 1.3, C.1, C.2 and the Set Splitting","+  transfer are standard in kind and short, and we checked only which","+  property of the counter they use. Whether each is correct as written","+  is the manuscript's, not ours.","+\\item \\textbf{Nothing about what the verified exponent would yield","+  through Corollary~1.4's argument.} A reader can rerun Appendix~C.1","+  with $a(t)$ replaced by the verified rate; we did not, because